// ============================================================
// Terraclima — summaryImmersive Length Polish
// ============================================================
//
// Expands summaryImmersive paragraphs that shipped under the ~80-150
// word target band. Each expansion appends new sentences grounded in
// the place's own biome, drivers, koppen class, and risk notes — the
// original authored sentence(s) are kept verbatim and extended, never
// replaced. Applied at load time in places.ts as an overlay that only
// replaces summaryImmersive when the authored text is still present
// as a prefix (so a later hand-edit of the base copy is never silently
// overridden by this stale expansion).
// ============================================================

export const SUMMARY_IMMERSIVE_POLISH: Record<string, { original: string; expanded: string }> = {
  "ajijic-lake-chapala-mx": { original: "Ajijic sits on Lake Chapala's north shore at a highland elevation that trims tropical heat while the lake softens daily extremes. The dry season is bright and warm; the wet season arrives with afternoon storms that green the surrounding slopes. Compared with Guadalajara, the lakeshore is smaller, breezier, and more temperature-stable, which is why it has become one of Mexico's best-known year-round comfort climates.", expanded: "Ajijic lies on the north shore of Lake Chapala, Mexico's largest lake, at about 1530 m, with the Sierra de San Juan Cosalá rising directly behind the village. Altitude takes the edge off the tropical sun and the lake evens out day and night. Winter afternoons reach about 25°C and nights fall to about 8°C. May is the hottest month, near 32°C, and then the rains arrive: most of the year's 780 mm or so falls from June to September, usually in evening storms that turn the hillsides green. The village is smaller and breezier than Guadalajara, an hour to the north, and has a large community of foreign retirees." },
  "alamos-mx": { original: "Álamos sits in the foothills of the Sierra Madre Occidental at 406 m. The North American Monsoon pushes moisture here reliably from July through September, transforming the dry thornscrub into luxuriant green. Winters are warm, dry, and bright — one of the mildest winter climates in northern Mexico.", expanded: "Álamos sits in the foothills of the Sierra Madre Occidental at about 400 m, a colonial silver town surrounded by tropical dry forest. For most of the year the hills are grey and leafless. The monsoon reaches them in July, and within weeks the forest is green; July to September brings about three-quarters of the year's 400 mm of rain. Winters are dry and bright, with afternoons near 27°C and nights near 9°C, which is when most visitors come. May and June are the hard months: afternoons of 36–38°C and no rain. Drought and summer heat are the things to plan around." },
  "anchorage-ak": { original: "Cook Inlet’s tides and sea ice modulate winter temperatures; Chugach orographics enhance snowfall on the east side. Summer days are long and mild; smoke from interior fires occasionally pools.", expanded: "Anchorage stands at the head of Cook Inlet with the Chugach Mountains directly behind it. The inlet keeps winter milder than interior Alaska, with afternoons near −4°C and nights near −11°C, and the mountains catch much of the Gulf of Alaska's rain, so the city gets only about 430 mm of precipitation a year. Snow totals about 200 cm, more on the hillside neighbourhoods to the east. Summer is long-lit and cool, with afternoons near 19°C, and August and September are the wettest months. Winter darkness is a bigger adjustment than the cold for most newcomers, and smoke from interior wildfires drifts in during dry summers." },
  "apalachicola-fl": { original: "Apalachicola sits where the Apalachicola River meets its eponymous bay — one of the most productive estuaries on the Gulf. The climate is subtropical but the marine moderation and onshore breezes keep summer afternoons below the Deep-South interior inferno. Oyster reefs, tupelo-honey swamps, and pine flatwoods together paint a distinctly regional picture.", expanded: "Apalachicola sits where the Apalachicola River enters its bay, behind a chain of barrier islands on the Florida Panhandle. The bay and the Gulf breeze keep summer afternoons near 32°C, a little below the inland Panhandle, though nights stay warm and humid at about 23°C. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 19°C and an occasional frost. Rain is heavy, about 1530 mm a year, and August and September are the wettest months. Tupelo swamps, pine flatwoods and salt marsh surround the town. The serious risk is hurricane storm surge on very low ground: Hurricane Michael came ashore just to the west in 2018." },
  "asheville-nc": { original: "Asheville sits at 650 m in the French Broad basin. The elevation produces summers significantly milder than the Piedmont or deep South — July afternoons average around 28°C versus 32°C in Charlotte 200 km east. Winters are mild with occasional snow; the surrounding ridges receive much more. The cove forests, orchards, and mild climate have attracted people for 150 years.", expanded: "Asheville sits at about 650 m in the French Broad River basin, ringed by the Black Mountains and the Pisgah ridges. That height keeps summer afternoons near 28°C while the Piedmont to the east is several degrees hotter, and nights cool to about 16°C. Winters are short and changeable, with afternoons near 10°C and a few light snowfalls that melt quickly in town and linger on the ridges. Rain comes in every month and totals about 1170 mm. Flooding is the main hazard: the French Broad and the Swannanoa run through the low parts of the city, and Hurricane Helene's rain in September 2024 flooded whole riverside districts." },
  "astoria-or": { original: "Astoria perches at the Columbia's mouth where river, ocean, and coastal mountains collide. The annual temperature range is among the continent's narrowest — winter highs of 9°C and summer highs of 20°C. Sunshine is rare (fewer than 70 sunny days a year); fog and marine cloud dominate. The place has the soul of a storm-lashed port because that is literally what it is.", expanded: "Astoria stands at the mouth of the Columbia River, where the river, the Pacific and the Coast Range meet. The ocean holds the temperature in a narrow band: winter afternoons near 10°C, summer afternoons near 20°C. Rain is the main fact of life. About 1790 mm falls in a year, and November, December and January each bring about 275 mm, while July gets under 20 mm. Low cloud and fog are common even in summer, and winter storms come straight off the Pacific across the Columbia Bar. People who like a working port, big weather and green hills do well here. People who need sun do not." },
  "austin-tx": { original: "Austin’s climate is a blend of Gulf moisture, plateau dry lines, and urban expansion that strengthens nocturnal heat. Hill-country storms fire on boundaries between air masses; flash flooding along the Colorado is a recurring story.", expanded: "Austin lies on the Balcones Escarpment, where the limestone Hill Country meets the coastal plain and humid Gulf air meets dry air from the west. Summer is long and hot: afternoons average about 36°C from June to August and nights stay near 23°C. Winter is brief and mild, with afternoons near 18°C, broken every few weeks by a sharp cold front. About 880 mm of rain falls in a year, with peaks in May and October, and it often comes in bursts. Creeks along the escarpment can flood within hours, and multi-year droughts draw down the Highland Lakes. The spring-fed pool at Barton Springs stays cool all summer." },
  "bacalar-mx": { original: "Bacalar sits on a freshwater lagoon famous for the way karst-leached limestone produces cyan, turquoise, and deep-blue water in distinct layers. The climate is tropical-isothermal: highs 30–33°C, lows 17–23°C, and small annual variation. Dry-season trade winds keep the humidity tolerable; wet season brings afternoon thunderstorms and occasional hurricane remnants from either coast.", expanded: "Bacalar sits beside a long freshwater lagoon in the limestone of the southern Yucatán Peninsula. Groundwater filtered through the rock gives the water its bands of pale turquoise and deep blue. The climate hardly changes through the year: afternoons run 30–34°C and nights 18–23°C. The dry season, from about December to April, has steady easterly trade winds that make the heat easier to bear. The rains come from June to October and bring most of the year's 1170 mm or so, in heavy bursts between hot, humid spells. Those are also the hurricane months. Heat and humidity in summer are the main thing to weigh." },
  "baie-saint-paul-qc": { original: "Baie-Saint-Paul sits where the Gouffre River reaches the broad St. Lawrence estuary, backed immediately by the Charlevoix hills. Summer is gentler than inland Quebec because estuary air trims heat; winter remains snowy and cold as storms lift into the Laurentians. The result is a compact cultural landscape of river meadows, apple pockets, forested slopes, and bright shoulder seasons that feel more coastal than the map suggests.", expanded: "Baie-Saint-Paul sits where the Gouffre River meets the St. Lawrence estuary, with the Charlevoix hills rising directly behind the town. Cold estuary water keeps summer gentler than inland Quebec, with afternoons near 23°C and cool nights. Winter is long and snowy: afternoons average about −4°C, nights about −13°C, and around 280 cm of snow falls as storms are lifted into the Laurentians. Precipitation is spread through the year and totals about 1020 mm. The valley floor holds river meadows and a few orchards, the slopes are forested, and the town has drawn painters for more than a century. Winter cold and storms are the costs." },
  "banff-ab": { original: "Banff's climate is mountain continental: long cold winters, mild short summers, and dramatic chinook warm spells that erase snowpack overnight. Precipitation is moderate and favors summer convection; shoulder seasons are crisp and dry.", expanded: "Banff sits at 1383 m in the Bow Valley, inside the front ranges of the Rockies. Winters are long and cold, with afternoons near −3°C and nights near −13°C, but chinook winds break them up several times a season with a few days of thaw. Summer is short and mild: afternoons reach about 21°C and nights drop to about 6°C, with frost possible in any month. The valley is fairly dry, about 460 mm a year, and June is the wettest month. The frost-free season is only about 90 days. Wildfire smoke in late summer and deep cold snaps in winter are the main things to plan around." },
  "bar-harbor-me": { original: "Bar Harbor sits on Mount Desert Island where sea fog rolls in on summer afternoons and fall colors are electric against dark granite. Winter is cold but moderated relative to interior Maine thanks to water; nor'easters bring heavy mixed precipitation.", expanded: "Bar Harbor sits on Mount Desert Island on the Maine coast, beside Acadia National Park. The Gulf of Maine is cold all year, so summer afternoons reach only about 24°C and sea fog often moves in when warm air crosses the water. Winter is cold, with afternoons near 1°C and nights near −8°C, though milder than inland Maine, and nor'easters bring heavy snow, rain or a mix of both. About 1370 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most from October to December. Early autumn is the clearest season, when the hardwoods turn colour against dark granite. Coastal storms are the main hazard." },
  "beverly-shores-in": { original: "Beverly Shores is where continental air masses first feel the thermal mass of Lake Michigan: delayed spring warming, abundant winter cloud when cold air crosses open water, and frequent afternoon wind shifts on stable summer days. The microclimate supports boreal relicts and rare orchids in protected swales — a biogeographic fingerprint of the lake.", expanded: "Beverly Shores sits among the dunes at the southern end of Lake Michigan, inside Indiana Dunes National Park. The lake delays spring, cools summer afternoons with an onshore breeze and keeps autumn mild. In winter, cold north winds crossing the open water bring lake-effect cloud and about 190 cm of snow a year. Summer afternoons average about 27°C and winter nights about −6°C, and about 1000 mm of precipitation falls through the year. The dunes, wet hollows and oak woods lie within walking distance of each other, and the cool, damp hollows hold northern plants and orchids that are rare elsewhere in Indiana. Severe thunderstorms are the main weather risk." },
  "bismarck-nd": { original: "Bismarck sits in one of the continent’s starkest annual temperature ranges. Arctic highs in winter deliver brilliant cold and dry air; summer opens to dryline thunderstorms and occasional severe hail. Chinook warming on eastern Rocky lee slopes occasionally interrupts mid-winter cold. Lake Sakakawea moderates lee-side microclimates only slightly — continentality dominates.", expanded: "Bismarck lies in the Missouri River valley on the northern Great Plains, about as far from an ocean as a North American city gets. The seasons are far apart: summer afternoons average about 28°C and winter nights about −15°C, and Arctic air can push readings far lower under clear, dry skies. Winter is sunny and cold, and the snow is dry and powdery. Most of the year's 490 mm of precipitation comes from May to July, much of it in thunderstorms that can bring hail. Now and then a chinook reaches this far east and lifts a winter day above freezing. Cold and drought are the two risks to weigh." },
  "block-island-ri": { original: "Block Island is small enough that every road is within a mile of water. That geometry means marine air dominates; heat waves are muted relative to Hartford or even Providence. Winter storms are oceanic and windy; summer is mild with persistent humidity. The island’s conservation lands preserve a rare Atlantic coastal grassland–heath mosaic tuned to salt spray.", expanded: "Block Island lies about 15 km off the Rhode Island coast, and no part of it is far from the sea. The ocean holds summer afternoons to about 25°C when the mainland is several degrees hotter, and keeps winter nights near −3°C. Summer air is humid and often foggy. Winter storms arrive with strong wind. About 1230 mm of precipitation falls fairly evenly through the year. Much of the island is protected land: open moors, shrub thickets and coastal grassland shaped by wind and salt spray. Coastal storms and erosion of the bluffs are the main hazards, and everything arrives by ferry or small plane." },
  "boone-nc": { original: "Boone perches at 995 m on the Blue Ridge Escarpment, high enough that its climate resembles New Hampshire more than the Carolina Piedmont below. Summer afternoons stay in the low-to-mid 20s °C; winters bring snow and real cold; cloudy-windy days are common. The Appalachian Trail passes through; the landscape carries an ecological character more northern than its latitude suggests.", expanded: "Boone sits at about 1000 m on the Blue Ridge of North Carolina, high enough that its weather resembles New England more than the Piedmont below. Summer afternoons average about 25°C and nights about 15°C. Winter brings real cold and about 140 cm of snow, with afternoons near 7°C and nights near −4°C. The ridge catches moisture from both the Gulf and the Atlantic, so about 1530 mm of precipitation falls in a year, and cloudy, windy days are common. Northern hardwoods and spruce grow on the higher slopes. Heavy-rain floods and landslides on steep ground are the main hazards, as Hurricane Helene showed in 2024." },
  "borrego-springs-ca": { original: "Borrego Springs sits in a bowl ringed by 2000 m peaks that cast a deep rain shadow. Winters are glorious: 22°C afternoons, 8°C nights, clear sky after clear sky. Summers are apocalyptic — 42°C is normal, dew points often below 10 °C, and the only reasonable activity is waiting for dark. The International Dark-Sky Community designation reflects that: no light leaks from here to anywhere.", expanded: "Borrego Springs lies in a desert valley about 180 m above sea level, walled in by the Santa Rosa and Vallecito mountains inside Anza-Borrego Desert State Park. The mountains block Pacific storms, and only about 150 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it in winter. Winter is the season to be here, with afternoons near 21°C, nights near 7°C and one clear day after another. Summer afternoons average about 39°C and July reaches about 41°C, so daily life moves to the early morning and after dark. The town keeps its lights low and is a certified dark-sky community. Water comes from a declining aquifer." },
  "boulder-co": { original: "Boulder's climate is defined by descent. When westerly winds cross the Divide, they compress and warm on the lee side, producing sudden 20°C warm-ups mid-winter. Snow vanishes within a day; the sun feels stronger than its latitude suggests. Summers are dry and warm, with afternoon convective thunderstorms climbing the range.", expanded: "Boulder sits at 1655 m where the Great Plains meet the foothills of the Colorado Front Range. When westerly winds cross the Continental Divide, the air warms as it comes down the eastern slope, and a winter day can warm by 20°C in a few hours. Snow rarely lasts more than a few days in town. Winter afternoons average about 9°C and nights about −5°C. Summer is warm and dry, with afternoons near 30°C, cool nights and thunderstorms building over the mountains. About 490 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it in April and May. The downslope winds can be destructive, and wildfire and smoke are real risks." },
  "bozeman-mt": { original: "Bozeman sits at 1474 m in the Gallatin Valley, a foothill basin between the Bridgers, Gallatins, and Tobacco Roots. Chinooks punctuate winters; diurnal swings are significant; sunshine is abundant. Summer afternoons reach 27–29°C but seldom higher thanks to the elevation. The climate is drier and sunnier than Montana's western valleys and cooler-summer than its eastern plains.", expanded: "Bozeman sits at 1474 m in the Gallatin Valley, with the Bridger Range to the northeast and the Gallatin Range to the south. The air is dry and the sun strong. Summer afternoons average about 27°C and nights fall to about 9°C. Winter is cold, with afternoons near 2°C and nights near −9°C, and it is broken now and then by a chinook. About 480 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it in May and June, and about 140 cm of snow. The frost-free season is short, about four months. Wildfire smoke in late summer is the most common complaint." },
  "broken-bow-ok": { original: "Broken Bow and the Mountain Fork corridor punch above the surrounding plains in annual precipitation and forest cover. Upslope flow ahead of Gulf lows enhances rainfall; reservoirs and rivers add local moisture. Summers remain hot, but canopy and shade produce perceptible relief compared to open pasture.", expanded: "Broken Bow lies in the foothills of the Ouachita Mountains in the southeast corner of Oklahoma, in pine and oak forest that the rest of the state lacks. Moist air from the Gulf is lifted over the hills, and about 1400 mm of rain falls in a year, with the most in April and May. Summer is hot and humid, with afternoons near 33°C and nights near 21°C, though the forest shade and the cold water of the Mountain Fork River give some relief. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 13°C and nights close to freezing. Flooding, summer heat and severe thunderstorms are the main risks." },
  "brookings-or": { original: "Brookings is already mild for the Oregon coast, but its claim to fame is episodic winter warmth: when pressure patterns align, air descends through the Chetco River corridor and compresses, producing rapid jumps that can melt frost in minutes while Astoria shivers. Baseline climate is still hyper-maritime — cool wet winters, foggy Junes, and summer highs that rarely tax a sweater.", expanded: "Brookings lies at the mouth of the Chetco River on the southern Oregon coast. Its usual weather is that of the outer coast: cool, wet winters, foggy early summers and summer afternoons of only about 20°C. About 2130 mm of rain falls in a year, almost all of it from October to April. What sets the town apart is the Chetco effect. When high pressure sits inland, air flows down the river valley toward the sea and warms as it descends, and Brookings can be the warmest place in Oregon on a winter day. Winter nights average about 5°C, and frost is uncommon. Pacific storms are the main hazard." },
  "buffalo-ny": { original: "Buffalo’s reputation is snow, but the mechanism is physics: cold air over warm open water, convection, and shoreline convergence. Summer benefits from lake cooling and convection along the breeze front.", expanded: "Buffalo sits at the eastern end of Lake Erie, at the downwind end of the lake's long axis. In late autumn and early winter, cold west winds cross the open water and pile snow onto the city and the towns just south of it in narrow, intense bands. About 270 cm falls in an average year, and one storm can bring a metre. The snow eases once the shallow lake freezes. Winter afternoons average about 2°C and nights about −6°C. The same lake cools summer, with afternoons near 26°C, and delays the first frost in autumn. About 1080 mm of precipitation falls through the year." },
  "burkes-garden-va": { original: "Burkes Garden is a geomorphic sink surrounded by continuous ridges. On clear, calm nights, katabatic drainage fills the bowl with dense air that cannot easily exit; the result is systematically lower minima than surrounding slopes at similar elevation. Agronomists and orchardists treat the floor and rim as different climates; atmospheric scientists use the basin as an eastern analog to western basin cold pools.", expanded: "Burkes Garden is an oval basin at about 920 m in the mountains of southwest Virginia, enclosed by one continuous ridge with a single gap. On clear, calm nights cold air drains off the ridge and fills the bowl, so the valley floor is colder at dawn than the slopes around it and frost comes later in spring and earlier in autumn. Summer is cool for Virginia, with afternoons near 26°C and nights near 14°C. Winter afternoons average about 6°C and nights about −5°C, with about 150 cm of snow. The basin is pasture and hay land with few services, and about 1270 mm of precipitation falls in a year." },
  "burlington-vt": { original: "Burlington gets lake-effect snow early in winter when the lake is still unfrozen; spring 'fifth season' fog is famous. Summer afternoons are milder than Boston or Hartford for latitude thanks to lake breezes. Fall color arrives with crisp Canadian air behind cold fronts.", expanded: "Burlington sits on the eastern shore of Lake Champlain, facing the Adirondacks across the water. The lake delays the first frost and gives early-winter snow squalls while it is still unfrozen, and a lake breeze cools summer afternoons, which average about 26°C. Winter is cold: afternoons hover around freezing and nights average about −10°C. About 1030 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in June and July, and the frost-free season runs a little over five months. Autumn colour arrives with the first clear, cold air from Canada. Spring is slow and muddy, and the lake sometimes floods low ground along the shore." },
  "campeche-mx": { original: "Campeche receives summer deluges from tropical waves and autumn hurricanes; winter is warm and pleasant. Sea breezes moderate the heat but not the humidity. The low flat plain means surge and flood risk during strong storms.", expanded: "Campeche is a walled colonial port on the Gulf of Mexico side of the Yucatán Peninsula, built on a low, flat coastal plain. It is hot all year. Afternoons average about 30°C in winter and reach about 36°C in May, before the rains, and winter nights average about 18°C. About 1080 mm of rain falls in a year, nearly all of it from June to October, and March and April are almost completely dry. A sea breeze eases the afternoon heat but not the humidity. The ground is so low that hurricane storm surge and flooding are the serious risks." },
  "cape-may-nj": { original: "Cape May is a classic barrier-style energy balance problem: water on three sides, low elevation, and tree canopy that knits humidity into every summer afternoon. Autumn can deliver warm ocean swimming while inland New Jersey cools; winter alternates between mild marine air and biting Arctic outbreaks when continental highs wedge behind the Appalachians.", expanded: "Cape May stands at the southern tip of New Jersey, with Delaware Bay on one side and the Atlantic on the other. Water on three sides slows every change of season. Spring is cool and late, summer afternoons average about 29°C with a sea breeze and humid nights, and the ocean stays warm enough for swimming into September. Winter is mild for the region, with afternoons near 8°C and nights near −1°C, though Arctic air breaks through a few times a season. About 1140 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. The town is barely above sea level, and coastal flooding from nor'easters and hurricanes is the main risk." },
  "charleston-sc": { original: "Charleston's low-country location on the Ashley-Cooper estuary produces humid-subtropical summers softened by reliable sea-breeze development by early afternoon — enough to bring 2–4°C of relief on most days compared to inland South Carolina. Winters are mild; frost is infrequent. Hurricanes are the existential climate factor; the statistical path brings major storms every decade or two.", expanded: "Charleston sits on a low peninsula where the Ashley and Cooper rivers meet the Atlantic. Summer is long, hot and humid, with afternoons near 30°C and nights that stay near 25°C. A sea breeze usually arrives by early afternoon and keeps the city a few degrees cooler than inland South Carolina. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 16°C and only an occasional frost. About 1280 mm of rain falls in a year, most heavily in July and August. Much of the city is barely above high tide, so some streets flood on the highest tides of the year, and hurricanes are the major risk." },
  "charlottetown-pei": { original: "PEI is a sandbar in the Gulf: winters are snowy and windy; summers are mild with persistent breezes that keep beaches comfortable. Spring fog is common; fall is peak tourism for harvest and lighthouses.", expanded: "Charlottetown is the capital of Prince Edward Island, a low island of red soil in the Gulf of St. Lawrence. The Gulf sets the seasons. It freezes in winter, so spring is late and foggy while the ice melts, and it warms slowly, so summer is mild, with afternoons near 23°C and steady breezes. Autumn stays mild longer than on the mainland. Winter is snowy and windy, with afternoons just below freezing and nights near −8°C. About 1170 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in October and November. Autumn storms, including the remains of hurricanes, are the main hazard on a low shoreline." },
  "chattanooga-tn": { original: "Chattanooga sits where the Tennessee River cuts a gap through plateau country, creating scenic overlooks and localized wind channeling. Summers are hot and humid; spring and fall bring severe thunderstorms along frontal boundaries. Winter is short with occasional light snow on surrounding ridges.", expanded: "Chattanooga lies where the Tennessee River cuts through the Cumberland Plateau, with Lookout Mountain and Signal Mountain standing above the city. Summer is hot and humid, with afternoons near 31°C and nights near 19°C, and the valley holds heat and haze on still days. Winter is short and mild, with afternoons near 12°C and nights just above freezing, and snow is light and mostly on the ridges. About 1450 mm of rain falls in a year, with the most from December to March. Spring and late autumn bring severe thunderstorms and the occasional tornado. The plateau tops, 300 m or more above the river, are noticeably cooler." },
  "churchill-mb": { original: "Churchill sits on Hudson Bay's west shore at 58.8° latitude. Hudson Bay freezes fully in winter, producing continental-cold conditions with lows below −30°C; during the open-water season (July–November), it acts as a heat-and-moisture source, moderating autumn temperatures and generating lake-effect-style snow. Polar bears gather here in October, waiting for the bay to re-freeze. Summer is brief, cool, and buggy.", expanded: "Churchill sits on the west shore of Hudson Bay at nearly 59° north, where the boreal forest gives way to tundra. The bay freezes over completely in winter, and with no open water nearby the cold is severe: afternoons average about −19°C and nights about −27°C. Summer is brief, cool and buggy, with afternoons near 16°C. From July to November the bay is open and it holds back the autumn chill and feeds snow squalls along the shore. Polar bears gather near town in October and November, waiting for the ice to form. Only about 350 mm of precipitation falls in a year. There is no road in." },
  "clayton-ga": { original: "Clayton is far enough south to feel subtropical in spring, but elevation and orographic cloudiness on moist southerly flow keep midsummer heat human. Waterfalls and north-facing coves stay shockingly cool; south-facing ridges open to sun for viticulture experiments. The contrast with Atlanta’s heat island is not subtle — it is a different regional climate at the same latitude band.", expanded: "Clayton lies at about 590 m in the northeast corner of Georgia, at the foot of the Blue Ridge. Moist air from the Gulf is forced up the mountain front, and about 1810 mm of rain falls in a year, which makes this one of the wettest parts of Georgia. Elevation and cloud keep summer afternoons near 29°C, a few degrees below Atlanta, and nights cool to about 17°C. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 11°C, nights around freezing and a little snow. Waterfalls and shaded north-facing coves stay cool all summer. Heavy rain is the main risk, bringing floods and landslides on steep ground." },
  "coatepec-mx": { original: "Coatepec sits on the windward Gulf slope of the Sierra Madre Oriental at 1250 m, where Atlantic moisture condenses reliably on hillside slopes. Coffee was introduced in the early 1800s and the town developed around an exceptional climate — cool, humid, mild, and perpetually misty.", expanded: "Coatepec sits at about 1250 m on the slope of the Sierra Madre Oriental that faces the Gulf of Mexico, in the coffee country below Xalapa. Moist air from the Gulf rises against the mountains and condenses here, so mist and drizzle are common in every season. About 1470 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from June to September. Temperatures are mild throughout: afternoons run from about 24°C in winter to 29°C in May, and nights from about 11°C to 16°C. Coffee has been grown under shade trees here since the early 1800s. Heavy rain brings floods and landslides on the steep slopes." },
  "cody-wy": { original: "Cody sits at the east edge of the Absaroka Range in the Bighorn Basin's rain shadow. The climate is dry (around 250 mm annual precipitation), sunny, and marked by dramatic chinook events: winter westerlies descending the Absarokas can lift temperatures 25°C in a day. Snow vanishes overnight. The basin's summers are warm but dry, and the proximity to Yellowstone makes the town a gateway.", expanded: "Cody lies at 1555 m at the western edge of the Bighorn Basin, in the rain shadow of the Absaroka Range. Only about 270 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it in May and June, and the country around town is sagebrush. Winter is sunny and windy. Chinook winds coming down off the Absarokas can raise the temperature 25°C in a day and clear the snow overnight, and winter afternoons average about 3°C. Summer is warm and dry, with afternoons near 28°C and nights near 11°C. Yellowstone's east entrance is about 85 km up the Shoshone River. Drought and wildfire smoke are the main risks." },
  "columbia-sc": { original: "Columbia sits just below the fall line: hotter summers than Charleston, colder winter snaps than the coast. The Congaree moderates some frost near the floodplain; urban expansion strengthens nocturnal heat.", expanded: "Columbia sits on the fall line of South Carolina, where the Piedmont drops to the sandy coastal plain and the Congaree River begins. It is too far inland for the sea breeze, so summer is hotter than on the coast: afternoons average about 33°C and nights about 21°C. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 15°C, but cold snaps are sharper than in Charleston and nights average about 2°C. About 1260 mm of rain falls in a year, most heavily in June and July. Heat and humidity from June to September are the main things to weigh, along with river flooding and the inland effects of hurricanes." },
  "crested-butte-co": { original: "Crested Butte sits 360 m above Gunnison in its own valley and yet — counterintuitively — often runs warmer on winter mornings because its ridgetop pocket sits above Gunnison's cold pool on inversion days. Summer is short, cool, and spectacular: July afternoon highs in the low 20s, a wildflower bloom unrivaled in the Rockies. Winters are deep, snowy, and magnificent.", expanded: "Crested Butte sits at 2707 m near the head of the East River valley, surrounded by peaks of about 3500 m. Winter is long and deep. About 400 cm of snow falls in a year, afternoons average about −3°C and nights about −20°C. Even so, on calm winter mornings the town is often warmer than Gunnison, 45 km down the valley, where the coldest air collects. Summer is short and cool, with afternoons near 23°C and nights near 3°C, and July is known for wildflower meadows. Frost can come in any month and the frost-free season is about 74 days. Altitude and winter cold are the things to weigh." },
  "creston-bc": { original: "Creston sits in a broad valley between two great ranges. Partial rain shadow, long summer days, fertile silt soils, and extensive wetlands support a steady fruit and grain economy.", expanded: "Creston lies at about 600 m in a broad valley between the Selkirk and Purcell mountains, at the south end of Kootenay Lake. The ranges to the west take some of the rain, and the valley gets about 630 mm of precipitation a year, most of it from November to January. Summer is warm and fairly dry, with afternoons near 27°C and cool nights. Winter is mild for the British Columbia interior, with afternoons just above freezing and nights near −5°C. Deep silt soils and long summer days support cherries, apples and grain, and the valley floor holds a large wetland. Wildfire smoke is the main summer drawback." },
  "cuauhtemoc-mx": { original: "Cuauhtémoc sits on the central Chihuahuan plateau at 2060 m. Mennonite settlers from Canada brought their dryland apple expertise here in the 1920s and developed what is now the country's largest apple-growing region. The climate is ideal for the fruit: chill hours are adequate, summers dry and sunny, nights cool enough for skin color development. The winter can bring genuine cold and occasional snow.", expanded: "Cuauhtémoc sits at 2060 m on the high plateau of central Chihuahua. Mennonite farmers who came from Canada in the 1920s settled the country around it, and it is now Mexico's main apple-growing district. The climate suits the trees. Winter is cold enough for them to rest, with nights around freezing and occasional snow, and summer is sunny, with afternoons near 30°C and cool nights. About 500 mm of rain falls in a year, more than half of it in July and August, and spring is very dry. Orchards rely on wells, and a falling water table and drought are the main long-term risks." },
  "cuernavaca-mx": { original: "Cuernavaca occupies a south-facing bench on the volcanic belt's flank, 1500 m above the tropical lowlands. Aztec emperors and Spanish viceroys retreated here for the climate: afternoons in the mid-to-high 20s year-round, nights cooling into the mid-teens, bougainvillea spilling off every wall. The rainy season arrives in June with dramatic afternoon convection off the volcanoes above, washing the air clean each evening. Winters are dry, bright, and warm.", expanded: "Cuernavaca sits at about 1500 m on the south-facing slope of Mexico's volcanic belt, below the high country around Mexico City. Aztec rulers and Spanish viceroys came here for the climate. Afternoons are warm in every month, from about 27°C in winter to 31°C in May, and nights range from about 11°C to 16°C. Winter and spring are dry and bright. The rains begin in June and run through September, usually as storms that build in the afternoon and clear by night, and they bring most of the year's 970 mm or so. Bougainvillea and jacaranda grow in every garden. Weekend traffic from the capital is the usual complaint." },
  "dawson-city-yt": { original: "Dawson City sits far inland near the Arctic Circle, where winter cold pools along the Yukon River and summer daylight runs long enough to push gardens fast. It is drier than many coastal northern places and much more seasonal: dark severe winters, dusty thaw, mosquito-rich green-up, and a brilliant fall that arrives early.", expanded: "Dawson City sits at the meeting of the Klondike and Yukon rivers, about 280 km south of the Arctic Circle. Cold air settles in the river valley in winter: afternoons average about −17°C and nights about −26°C, and the sun barely clears the hills in December. Summer is short and surprisingly warm, with afternoons near 22°C and daylight almost around the clock, and gardens grow fast. Only about 320 mm of precipitation falls in a year. The frost-free season is under three months, and autumn colour arrives by late August. Winter cold, isolation and summer wildfire smoke are the things to weigh." },
  "des-moines-ia": { original: "Des Moines sits in America’s most productive corn belt — summer dew points and CAPE build ahead of fronts; winter brings Arctic outbreaks and occasional ice storms.", expanded: "Des Moines sits where the Raccoon River joins the Des Moines River, on rolling farmland in the middle of the corn belt. No mountain or large lake moderates it, so the seasons are far apart. Summer is hot and humid, with afternoons near 29°C and nights near 17°C, and Gulf moisture feeds strong thunderstorms from May to July, the wettest part of a year that totals about 920 mm. Winter is cold, with afternoons just above freezing and nights near −9°C, and Arctic air and the odd ice storm arrive several times a season. River flooding and severe storms, including tornadoes and straight-line winds, are the main risks." },
  "duluth-mn": { original: "Duluth's elevation above the lake and persistent onshore flow keeps July afternoons cooler than Minneapolis by a wide margin. Winter brings legendary lake-effect snow bands on the South Shore. Spring is slow to arrive — fog and ice linger — but autumn is crisp and brilliant.", expanded: "Duluth climbs a steep hillside at the western tip of Lake Superior. The lake is cold well into summer, and a wind off the water can drop the temperature near the shore by 10°C in minutes, so forecasts here often end with the words “cooler by the lake”. Summer afternoons average about 23°C. Winter is long and cold, with afternoons near −4°C and nights near −12°C, and the neighbourhoods on top of the hill get more snow than the harbour. Spring comes late, with ice and fog lingering on the lake, and autumn is clear and bright. About 760 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in June." },
  "durango-co": { original: "Durango sits at 1988 m in the Animas Valley, sheltered between the San Juan peaks to the north and the Mesa Verde country to the south. The valley orientation moderates extremes; winters are mild by Rocky Mountain standards (January mean high around 5°C) and summers stay in the high 20s. Monsoon season brings dramatic afternoon thunderstorms against the peaks.", expanded: "Durango sits at 1988 m in the Animas River valley, with the San Juan Mountains to the north and the mesa country of the Colorado Plateau to the south. Winters are sunny and mild for the Rockies, with afternoons near 6°C, nights near −9°C and about 140 cm of snow. Summer afternoons reach about 30°C and nights cool to about 11°C. The monsoon arrives in July, and August and September are the wettest months of a year that totals about 470 mm; storms build over the peaks most afternoons. May and June are dry, and that is when wildfire danger is highest. Smoke and drought are the main risks." },
  "durango-mx": { original: "Durango is not tropical Mexico. Its elevation keeps nights crisp, the dry season is bright and dusty, and summer storms build off the Sierra Madre Occidental. The city sits in a broad basin, so stagnant winter mornings and strong day-night swings both belong to the lived climate.", expanded: "Durango lies at 1880 m in a broad valley east of the Sierra Madre Occidental. This is the dry northern highland, not tropical Mexico. Winter days are sunny and warm, near 22°C, but nights fall to about 3°C and light frost is common. Spring is dry and dusty, and May is the hottest month, with afternoons near 31°C. The rains come in July and August, when storms build over the sierra and drift across the valley in the afternoon; they bring about half of the year's 440 mm. Nights are more than 15°C cooler than the afternoons in most months. Drought is the main risk." },
  "ellensburg-wa": { original: "Ellensburg sits at a classic gap: the Kittitas Valley funnels pressure differences between the wet west side and the dry Columbia Plateau into persistent, often howling wind. Summers are hot-dry-sunny and winters are cold but clearer than west of the crest. The wind is the signature — ranchers joke that the day it stops blowing everyone in town goes to see a doctor.", expanded: "Ellensburg sits in the Kittitas Valley on the dry side of the Cascades, where the Yakima River comes down from Snoqualmie Pass. Only about 270 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it in winter, and the unirrigated hills are sagebrush. The town is known for wind. Cool marine air west of the mountains pushes through the pass toward the hot interior, and in spring and summer it blows from the northwest for days at a time. Wind turbines line the ridges and the valley exports timothy hay. Summer afternoons average about 28°C and winter nights about −4°C, with about 80 cm of snow. Wildfire smoke is the main summer risk." },
  "eminence-mo": { original: "Eminence is the gateway to some of the clearest spring rivers in the Lower 48. Groundwater upwelling holds near-stable temperatures year-round; in summer, paddlers experience cold pools and fog banks at confluences that would not exist in a non-karst landscape. The hills are modest in elevation but enormous in hydrologic complexity.", expanded: "Eminence sits in the Ozark hills of southern Missouri, where the Jacks Fork meets the Current River. Both rivers are fed by large springs that come out of the limestone at about 14°C all year. On summer mornings that cold water lays fog along the valleys and cools the air beside the river, while the ridges above are hot. Summer afternoons average about 31°C and winter nights about −4°C. About 1210 mm of rain falls in a year, with the most in April and May. The rivers rise fast after heavy rain, and flooding is the main hazard. The town is small and a long drive from a city." },
  "erie-pa": { original: "Erie receives some of the heaviest snow totals in Pennsylvania when cold air crosses the warm lake. Summer is mild and humid with lake breezes; thunderstorms rake along the lake breeze front. The climate is a hybrid of Midwest continentality and Great Lakes maritime delay.", expanded: "Erie sits on the south shore of Lake Erie, sheltered by the sand spit of Presque Isle. In late autumn and early winter, cold winds crossing the open lake bring heavy snow to the city and even more to the higher ground just inland. A single storm can leave more than a metre. The snow eases if the lake freezes. Winter afternoons average about 2°C and nights about −5°C. The lake delays spring, cools summer afternoons to about 26°C and holds off the first autumn frost, which is why vineyards line the shore to the east. About 1150 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in autumn." },
  "friday-harbor-wa": { original: "The San Juans sit in a complex rain shadow cast by the Olympics and Vancouver Island — enough moisture for pastoral green, but enough summer sun to support vineyards and apple orchards on south slopes. Marine breezes keep August afternoons civilized; frost is rare near shore.", expanded: "Friday Harbor is the main town of San Juan Island, in the sheltered water between Vancouver Island and the Washington mainland. The Olympic Mountains and Vancouver Island take most of the rain from Pacific storms before they arrive, so the island gets about 460 mm of precipitation a year, less than half of Seattle's. Summers are dry, with afternoons near 21°C and a breeze off cold water. Winters are mild and grey, with afternoons near 8°C, and frost is light near the shore. Garry oak meadows and dry grassland cover the south-facing slopes. Fresh water is limited on the island, and everything arrives by ferry or small plane." },
  "galena-il": { original: "Galena’s postcard hills are not decorative; they organize cold drainage, spring phenology, and wind exposure in ways flat corn country cannot. The Mississippi moderates some winter cold but also supplies moisture for lake-effect-like cloud streets when synoptic flow aligns. It is a microclimate laboratory squeezed into a tourist town.", expanded: "Galena lies among steep hills and bluffs near the Mississippi River in the northwest corner of Illinois. This is the Driftless Area, which the glaciers that flattened the rest of the state did not cover. On clear nights cold air drains into the valleys, so frost dates and spring bloom differ between the valley floors, the slopes and the ridge tops. Summer is warm and humid, with afternoons near 28°C. Winter is cold, with afternoons around freezing, nights near −10°C and about 105 cm of snow. About 980 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in June and July. The Galena River has flooded the old town many times, and floodgates now protect it." },
  "gaspe-qc": { original: "The Gaspé's eastern tip plunges into the Gulf of St. Lawrence, which is cold for much of the year. Summers stay cool and frequently foggy; winter is long and stormy with wind-driven snow. The maritime modification softens what at 48° latitude inland would be brutally continental — making this one of Eastern Canada's most dramatic land-sea climate transitions.", expanded: "The town of Gaspé sits near the eastern tip of the Gaspé Peninsula, where the Appalachian ridges run out into the Gulf of St. Lawrence. The Gulf is cold for most of the year. Summer afternoons average about 22°C, nights are cool, and fog is common along the outer shore. Winter is long, windy and snowy: afternoons average about −4°C, nights about −14°C, and about 320 cm of snow falls. Even so, the sea makes it milder than inland Quebec at the same latitude. About 1200 mm of precipitation falls through the year. The frost-free season lasts about 130 days. Autumn and winter storms off the Gulf are the main hazard." },
  "gatlinburg-tn": { original: "Gatlinburg sits at the interface of tourist strip and national park rainforest. Orographic lifting on the Smokies produces abundant rainfall and persistent fog on cool mornings; elevation gradients mean wildflower and fall-color seasons are long and stratified by slope aspect.", expanded: "Gatlinburg sits at about 390 m in a narrow valley at the northern foot of the Great Smoky Mountains. Moist air rising over the range brings about 1500 mm of rain a year to the town and more than 2000 mm to the high ridges above it. Summer in the valley is warm and humid, with afternoons near 30°C, but every few hundred metres of climb into the national park brings cooler air. Winter is mild in town, with afternoons near 11°C, while the crest holds snow. Spring wildflowers and autumn colour move up and down the slopes over several weeks. Flash floods on the mountain streams are the main hazard." },
  "geneva-on-the-lake-oh": { original: "Geneva-on-the-Lake inherits the full Great Lakes curriculum: delayed leaf-out, abundant winter cloud, and sharp mesoscale snow bands that can bury the strip while Columbus stays dry. Summer is warm and humid with lake breezes; thunderstorms propagate along lake-outflow boundaries.", expanded: "Geneva-on-the-Lake is a small resort town on the Lake Erie shore of northeast Ohio. The lake governs the seasons. Spring arrives late, because the water is still cold. Summer afternoons average about 27°C and are cooled by a lake breeze. Autumn stays mild long enough to ripen the wine grapes grown just inland. From November to January, cold winds crossing the open lake bring bands of heavy snow, and about 240 cm falls in an average winter. Winter afternoons average about 3°C and nights about −5°C. About 1070 mm of precipitation falls in a year. The snow can be intense for a day or two and then stop a few kilometres down the road." },
  "grand-manan-nb": { original: "Twelve-meter Fundy tides cycle enormous volumes of cold water past the island, producing a dramatically moderated maritime climate: cool wet summers, mild winters, relentless fog, and a salt-scrubbed plant community.", expanded: "Grand Manan lies in the mouth of the Bay of Fundy, off the coast of New Brunswick. The bay's tides, among the largest in the world, move cold water past the island twice a day, and the sea stays cold all summer. Afternoons reach only about 22°C in July and August, and fog is frequent when warmer air drifts over the water. Winter is milder than on the mainland, with afternoons just above freezing and nights near −8°C. About 1340 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most from October to December. Islanders fish lobster and gather dulse. A ferry is the only regular link, and winter storms can stop it." },
  "grand-marais-mi": { original: "Winter winds cross 300 km of open Lake Superior before reaching this sandy shore, dumping colossal snow totals. Summers are short, cool, and exquisite; winters are defining. Communities along the Grand Marais–Munising shore measure snow in meters, not centimeters.", expanded: "Grand Marais is a small harbour town on the south shore of Lake Superior, in Michigan's Upper Peninsula. In winter, northwest winds cross more than 200 km of open water before they reach this shore, and they bring about 380 cm of snow a year. Winter afternoons average about −2°C and nights about −10°C. Summer is short and cool. Afternoons reach about 23°C, nights drop to about 11°C, and the lake is cold enough to chill the beach on any day with an onshore wind. About 920 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in September and October. The town is far from a hospital or an airport." },
  "guanajuato-mx": { original: "Guanajuato is built into a steep canyon at 2000 m in the Sierra de Guanajuato. Its geography produces fascinating microclimate variation along the canyon walls — sun-facing slopes warm quickly while shaded ones stay cool. The general climate is cool-dry eternal-spring: afternoon highs 22–27°C, dry most of the year with a June–September rainy season. The colorful terraced houses catch the high-altitude sun from every angle.", expanded: "Guanajuato is built along a narrow ravine at about 2000 m in the Sierra de Guanajuato, a silver-mining city of steep lanes and terraced houses. The slopes matter here: a house on the sunny side of the ravine warms quickly in the morning while the other side stays in shade. Afternoons are mild all year, from about 23°C in winter to 30°C in May, and winter nights fall to about 6°C. The year's 530 mm of rain comes mostly from June to September, in afternoon storms. The rest of the year is dry and sunny. Floods once ran down the ravine through the city, and the old riverbed is now a road tunnel." },
  "halifax-ns": { original: "Halifax mixes Gulf Stream–influenced mild winters with foggy springs and crisp autumns. Summer heat is moderated by onshore flow; winter snowfall is modest but blizzards can roar off the Atlantic. Hurricane remnants occasionally bring heavy rain.", expanded: "Halifax sits on a deep harbour on the Atlantic coast of Nova Scotia. The ocean keeps summer mild, with afternoons near 22°C, and it is warmest in August, later than inland. Winter swings between cold continental air and milder air off the sea, with afternoons near 2°C and nights near −7°C, and storms often change from snow to rain and back. Spring is slow and foggy. Autumn is the most settled season. About 1450 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in November and December. The remains of hurricanes reach this coast in some autumns, and Hurricane Juan did serious damage to the city in 2003." },
  "hermosillo-mx": { original: "Hermosillo is core desert: triple-digit afternoon heat indices for months, cooling at night when skies clear. July–August storms bring drama — virga, dust, localized downpours — but annual totals stay low. Winter is the pleasant season outsiders underestimate.", expanded: "Hermosillo lies in the Sonoran Desert about 100 km inland from the Gulf of California. Summer is very hot: afternoons average about 39°C from June to August and nights stay near 26°C. June is the hottest and driest month. In July and August moisture arrives from the south, bringing humidity, dust storms and scattered heavy downpours; July to September supplies about three-quarters of the year's 310 mm of rain. Winter is the comfortable season, with sunny afternoons near 25°C and nights near 12°C. The city depends on reservoirs and wells in a region where drought is common. Extreme heat from May to September is the main thing to weigh." },
  "honolulu-hi": { original: "Honolulu sits where trades split around topography; Waianae range shadows produce west-side dryness while Hilo-like rainfall stays windward. Urban heat island elevates minima in the core.", expanded: "Honolulu lies on the south shore of Oʻahu, in the lee of the Koʻolau Range. Trade winds from the northeast drop their rain on the windward side of the mountains, and the city gets only about 510 mm a year while the valleys behind it get several times as much. Temperature barely changes: afternoons run from about 27°C in winter to 30°C in late summer, and nights from 19°C to 23°C. The trades keep the humidity bearable on most days. Most rain comes between November and March, when storms from the south interrupt the trades. Flash floods, high surf and rising seas along a low shore are the main risks." },
  "hood-river-gorge": { original: "The Columbia Gorge is one of the world's great natural wind tunnels: the only sea-level gap through a major mountain range. West-to-east, it channels marine air inland, and east-to-west, it channels desert dryness outward — with the resulting pressure gradient producing the world-famous windsurfing winds. The climate transects here are staggering: from moss-dripping rainforest at Bonneville to sagebrush steppe 80 km east at The Dalles.", expanded: "The Columbia River Gorge is the only sea-level route through the Cascade Range. Cool marine air west of the mountains and hot, dry air to the east are always trying to trade places through it, and the result is strong, steady wind: from the west on most summer days, from the east in winter cold snaps. The climate changes quickly along the river. Cascade Locks gets about 1900 mm of rain a year and stands in mossy forest; The Dalles, about 65 km east, gets under 400 mm and is grassland. Summer afternoons in the middle of the gorge average about 30°C. Wildfire, smoke and winter ice storms are the main risks." },
  "houghton-mi": { original: "The Keweenaw thrusts 100 km into Lake Superior, exposing it to lake-effect snow from nearly every wind direction. Annual snowfall averages 5 m near Houghton and approaches 7 m on the peninsula's spine. The copper-mining history, the university at Michigan Tech, and the culture of the place are all shaped by the snow. Summers, by contrast, are idyllic: mild, breezy, and sun-sparkled.", expanded: "Houghton sits on the Keweenaw Peninsula, which reaches about 100 km out into Lake Superior. With water on three sides, winds from the west, north or east all cross the lake first, and almost any cold wind brings snow. About 550 cm falls in an average winter, and more on the high ground up the peninsula. Winter afternoons average about −3°C and nights about −11°C. Summer is mild, with afternoons near 24°C and cool nights. About 830 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Snow shapes the houses, the roads and the calendar of this old copper-mining district, which is now home to Michigan Technological University." },
  "international-falls-mn": { original: "International Falls anchors the border climate where Arctic air masses arrive unimpeded and winter nights routinely plunge far below freezing. Summer is brief but surprisingly pleasant — warm days, cool nights, long evening light. Lake-effect snow and lake ice modulate the harshest extremes slightly, but not enough to soften the reputation.", expanded: "International Falls sits on the Rainy River, facing Ontario, in flat forest and lake country. Nothing stands between it and the Arctic, and on calm winter nights cold air settles into the river lowland. Winter afternoons average about −7°C and nights about −18°C, and far colder spells come every year. The town trades on the nickname “Icebox of the Nation”. Summer is short and pleasant, with afternoons near 25°C, cool nights and long evenings. About 700 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it as summer rain. The frost-free season lasts about four months. Winter cold is the thing to weigh; little else here is extreme." },
  "jackson-wy": { original: "Jackson Hole traps cold air and captures Pacific moisture lifted by the Tetons; snowpack feeds the Snake River system that defines the ecosystem.", expanded: "Jackson sits at about 1900 m at the south end of Jackson Hole, a flat-floored valley between the Teton and Gros Ventre ranges. Pacific storms are lifted by the Tetons and leave deep snow on the mountains. Cold air collects on the valley floor in winter, when afternoons average about −1°C and nights about −13°C. Summer days are warm, near 26°C, but nights drop to about 6°C and frost can come in any month; the frost-free season is under three months. The valley floor gets only about 470 mm of precipitation a year. Late-summer wildfire smoke and winter cold are the main things to weigh, along with very high housing costs." },
  "joseph-or": { original: "The Wallowas rise 3000 m from broad glacial valleys in Oregon's remote northeast. Joseph and Enterprise sit in the Wallowa Valley at 1292 m — high, dry, sunny, and sheltered from both Pacific storms and the worst of Rocky Mountain cold. Summers are warm-dry-brilliant, winters bring reliable snow but without deep-cold continental intensity. The valley's ranching character is visible and the scenery is Alpine in both senses.", expanded: "Joseph sits at about 1290 m at the foot of the Wallowa Mountains in the far northeast of Oregon, beside the glacial basin of Wallowa Lake. The peaks behind it reach nearly 3000 m. The valley is dry and sunny, with about 440 mm of precipitation a year. Summer afternoons average about 26°C and nights cool to about 9°C. Winter brings about 160 cm of snow, with afternoons near 3°C and nights near −6°C, less severe than in the Rockies farther east. It is ranching country, and it is remote: the nearest interstate highway is more than an hour away. Wildfire and smoke in late summer are the main risks." },
  "kamloops-bc": { original: "Kamloops sits at the confluence of the North and South Thompson rivers in the deep Coast-Mountains rain shadow. Annual precipitation averages under 300 mm. Summer afternoons push into the 33–36°C range; the hillsides are sagebrush, bunchgrass, and ponderosa pine. Winters are cold-sunny and drier than elsewhere at the latitude. It's Canada's most American-Southwest-feeling small city.", expanded: "Kamloops sits where the North and South Thompson rivers meet, in the rain shadow of the Coast Mountains. It is one of the driest cities in Canada, with about 320 mm of precipitation a year, and the hills around it are sagebrush, bunchgrass and scattered ponderosa pine. Summer is hot and sunny: afternoons average about 28°C, and heat waves push well beyond that. Winter is cold but often clear, with afternoons just above freezing, nights near −5°C and only about 65 cm of snow. June is the wettest month. Wildfire and smoke are the serious risks in summer, and in some recent years smoke has filled the valley for weeks." },
  "kelowna-bc": { original: "Kelowna combines interior sunshine with lake thermal inertia: July afternoons are warm enough for Syrah and Merlot, but lake breezes knock the edge off heat domes. Winter inversions trap smog and moisture in the valley; spring frost fights are a viticulture ritual.", expanded: "Kelowna lies on the east shore of Okanagan Lake, in the dry interior valley behind the Coast and Cascade ranges. About 350 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Summer is warm and sunny, with afternoons near 28°C, and the lake takes a little heat out of the shore. Vineyards and orchards cover the benches above the water. Winter is mild for the interior, with afternoons near 3°C and nights near −3°C, but low cloud often fills the valley for days. Spring frost is the growers' main worry. Wildfire is the main risk for everyone else: fires in 2003 and 2023 burned into neighbourhoods on both sides of the lake." },
  "la-paz-mx": { original: "La Paz sits on a sheltered bay of the Sea of Cortez, where the desert coast meets some of the world's clearest water. Winters are nearly ideal — 26°C days, 14°C nights, constant sun. Summers are brutal: 35°C afternoons, humidity rising in the wet season, and hurricane season from August through October. The warm Cortez moderates summer heat compared to the interior but raises humidity.", expanded: "La Paz sits on a sheltered bay of the Gulf of California, near the southern end of the Baja California peninsula. Winter is the season people come for, with sunny afternoons near 26°C and nights near 12°C. Summer is hot, with afternoons near 37°C, and it turns humid from August as the Gulf warms. On many summer evenings a breeze known as the Coromuel crosses the peninsula from the Pacific and cools the city. Only about 160 mm of rain falls in a year, and nearly half of it comes in September, usually from a passing tropical storm. Water is scarce, and hurricanes are the main hazard." },
  "leamington-on": { original: "Leamington sits at 42° latitude — level with California's northern border — on Lake Erie's north shore. The combination of low latitude and lake moderation produces the most growing-season heat in Canada (a frost-free season of well over 180 days) and some of its warmest summers. The town has been the country's tomato-growing capital for generations; massive greenhouse complexes now dominate, leveraging both the climate and lake-sourced cooling/heating.", expanded: "Leamington lies on the north shore of Lake Erie at 42° north, the same latitude as northern California, beside Point Pelee, the southern tip of mainland Canada. Low latitude and the warm, shallow lake give it the warmest growing season in the country. Summer afternoons average about 26°C and nights about 17°C, and the frost-free season lasts about 195 days. Winter is mild for Ontario, with afternoons just above freezing and about 125 cm of snow. About 900 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. Field tomatoes made the town's name, and it now has one of the largest concentrations of greenhouses in North America. Summer thunderstorms are the main weather risk." },
  "leavenworth-wa": { original: "Leavenworth combines Cascade scenery with continental seasonality: summer heat breaks 30°C regularly; winter snow is reliable for recreation. Chinooks occasionally chew snowpack in midwinter. Irrigation from the Wenatchee supports apples and pears that need winter chill.", expanded: "Leavenworth sits at about 350 m in the Wenatchee River valley on the east slope of the Cascades. It is dry in summer and snowy in winter. Pacific storms that cross the crest leave most of the year's 650 mm of precipitation between November and February, much of it as snow, while July and August are nearly rainless. Summer afternoons average about 29°C and often pass 30°C, with cool nights. Winter afternoons average about 3°C and nights about −4°C. Pear and apple orchards fill the valley below town, watered from the river. Wildfire and smoke are the serious risks in late summer, and the valley has been evacuated more than once." },
  "lewes-de": { original: "Lewes sits where the bay’s huge tidal prism organizes daily wind: onshore flow on warm afternoons, land breezes at night, and periodic nor’easters that reshape coastal dunes. It lacks the elevation microclimates of the Appalachians but exemplifies estuarine moderation in a mostly flat state.", expanded: "Lewes stands where Delaware Bay opens to the Atlantic, behind the dunes of Cape Henlopen. Water on two sides evens out the seasons. Summer afternoons average about 29°C, with a sea breeze on most warm days and humid nights near 19°C. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 9°C, nights around freezing and about 40 cm of snow. About 1180 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. Spring warms slowly because the bay is cold, and autumn stays mild for the same reason. The town is only a few metres above sea level, and flooding from nor'easters and hurricanes is the main risk, growing as the sea rises." },
  "lone-pine-ca": { original: "Lone Pine lives in the hydraulic shadow of the Sierra: dry, windy, and often hazy from Owens Lake dust and seasonal smoke. Winter cold pools in the valley; summer convection builds over the peaks while the floor bakes. It is the gateway climate for Whitney Portal — roughly 1,000 vertical metres of lapse-rate cooling in minutes of driving.", expanded: "Lone Pine lies on the floor of the Owens Valley at 1130 m, directly below Mount Whitney and the highest part of the Sierra Nevada. The range takes nearly all the moisture from Pacific storms, and the valley floor is desert. Summer is hot, with afternoons near 36°C and nights near 19°C. Winter days are sunny and mild, near 14°C, with nights around freezing. The road to Whitney Portal climbs about 1400 m in 20 km, into air about 10°C cooler. Wind is frequent, and it raises dust from the dry bed of Owens Lake. Drought, summer heat and wildfire smoke are the main risks." },
  "lubbock-tx": { original: "Lubbock sits on the Llano Estacado where summer afternoons routinely top 35°C and relative humidity drops below 20%. Spring brings dryline thunderstorms and occasional dust storms. Winter is sunny and mild by northern standards but punctuated by blue northers.", expanded: "Lubbock sits at about 980 m on the Llano Estacado, the flat high plain of west Texas. Summer afternoons average about 34°C, but the air is dry and nights cool to about 19°C. Winter is sunny and mild, with afternoons near 14°C, until a cold front arrives and drops the temperature 15°C or more in a few hours. About 490 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from May to September in thunderstorms. Spring is the rough season, with strong wind, blowing dust, large hail and tornadoes. The cotton fields around the city are irrigated from the Ogallala Aquifer, which is being drawn down. Drought is a constant concern." },
  "mammoth-lakes-ca": { original: "Mammoth sits at 2424 m on the east side of the Sierra crest where storms deposit their snow before descending to the Owens Valley rain shadow. The climate is subalpine — 30 inches of water equivalent in an average winter, 300+ sunny days, intense UV, and summer afternoons that rarely exceed 25°C. The volcanic geology (still an active caldera) adds geothermal features that shape local micro-microclimates.", expanded: "Mammoth Lakes sits at 2424 m on the east side of the Sierra Nevada, at the edge of the Long Valley Caldera. A low point in the crest to the west lets Pacific storms through, and about 610 cm of snow falls in an average winter. Winter days are often sunny between storms, with afternoons near 6°C and nights near −8°C. Summer is dry and mild. Afternoons reach about 25°C, nights fall to about 7°C, and the frost-free season is about 105 days. Hot springs and steam vents in the caldera are reminders that the volcano is still active. Wildfire smoke, heavy snow and altitude are the things to plan for." },
  "mazamitla-mx": { original: "Mazamitla sits at 2220 m in the Sierra del Tigre. The climate is cool year-round; winter nights can approach freezing, summer afternoons rarely exceed 22°C.", expanded: "Mazamitla sits at 2220 m in the pine and oak forest of the Sierra del Tigre, south of Lake Chapala. It is cool for Mexico in every season. Winter afternoons reach about 23°C, and nights fall to about 5°C and sometimes to freezing. May is the warmest month, near 29°C. Then the rains arrive, and July and August alone bring about 400 mm of the year's 900 mm, with cool, cloudy afternoons. Houses have fireplaces and people use them. The town fills at weekends with visitors from Guadalajara, about two hours away, who come for wooden cabins and cold nights." },
  "mazatlan-mx": { original: "Mazatlán delivers postcard winters and steamy summers when the ITCZ influence returns. Pacific storms occasionally send swell and humidity; inland sierras wring rain that rarely reaches the beach uniformly.", expanded: "Mazatlán lies on the Pacific coast of Sinaloa, just south of the Tropic of Cancer, across from the tip of Baja California. Winter is dry and warm, with afternoons near 27°C and nights near 15°C, and almost no rain falls from February to May. Summer is hot and humid: afternoons average about 34°C and nights stay near 25°C. About 80% of the year's 610 mm of rain falls from July to September, much of it in evening thunderstorms. Hurricanes approach this coast in most years, usually in September and October, and they are the main hazard. Humid summer heat is the everyday cost." },
  "medford-or": { original: "Medford sits in a classic inter-mountain rain shadow: the Coast Range and Klamaths wring out Pacific moisture west and north, leaving the Rogue Basin with sunny, arid summers that feel more Central Valley than Portland. Winter inversions trap smoke and fog in the bowl; spring arrives abruptly with blossom corridors along irrigation canals.", expanded: "Medford lies on the floor of the Rogue Valley, with the Siskiyou and Klamath mountains to the west and south and the Cascades to the east. The mountains take much of the rain, and summers here are hot, dry and sunny, more like California's Central Valley than Portland. July afternoons reach about 34°C, though nights cool to about 14°C. Winter is mild and damp, with afternoons near 10°C, and fog can sit in the valley for days under an inversion. About 550 mm of rain falls in a year, almost all from October to May. Pear orchards made the valley's name. Wildfire and smoke are the serious risks from July to September." },
  "mentone-al": { original: "Mentone sits on the same Appalachian escarpment that runs into Georgia and Tennessee, but here the state’s stereotypical swelter finally breaks. Westerly storm systems ride the plateau edge; thermal belts and coves on the scarp produce sharp spring frost contrasts that orchardists still map by hand. It is one of the few places in Alabama where true four-season gardening feels honest.", expanded: "Mentone sits at about 550 m on the edge of Lookout Mountain in northeast Alabama, about 280 m above the valley to the west. The height takes a few degrees off the Alabama summer: afternoons average about 29°C and nights about 18°C. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 10°C, nights around freezing and a little snow. The plateau edge catches rain from passing storms, and about 1640 mm falls in a year, with the most from December to March. Summer camps have operated here for a century, and DeSoto Falls and Little River Canyon are nearby. Severe thunderstorms, tornadoes and flash floods are the main risks." },
  "merida-mx": { original: "Mérida sits on the Yucatán's flat limestone plateau, where cenotes (collapsed karst sinkholes) provide the region's only surface water. Winters are warm-dry-sunny (highs 30°C, low humidity); summers are hotter and humid with afternoon downpours from May through October. Hurricane exposure is real but the inland position dampens the worst direct impacts compared to Cancún or Campeche on the coast.", expanded: "Mérida stands on the flat limestone plain of the northern Yucatán, about 35 km inland from the Gulf of Mexico. There are no rivers; water lies underground and reaches the surface in cenotes, the sinkholes of the region. It is hot all year. Winter afternoons reach about 30°C with dry air and cooler nights near 17°C. April and May are the hottest months, near 36°C. Rain comes from June to October, in afternoon downpours that bring most of the year's 960 mm, and the humidity stays high. Being inland takes the worst edge off hurricanes, though not their rain. Summer heat is the thing to weigh." },
  "missoula-mt": { original: "Missoula's geometry — a broad valley ringed by mountains, five rivers converging, frequent calm high-pressure winter weather — produces reliable, stubborn inversions. On the worst weeks, stagnant cold air fills the valley to 400 m depth while ridgetops bask in sun 15°C warmer. Smoke events amplify the stagnation. Summers, by contrast, are warm, sunny, and smoke-smudged increasingly often as fires climb nearby canyons.", expanded: "Missoula lies at 975 m where the Clark Fork, Bitterroot and Blackfoot valleys come together, ringed by mountains. In winter, cold air settles on the valley floor under a lid of warmer air, and fog and wood smoke can stay for a week or more while the ridges above are sunny. Winter afternoons average about 2°C and nights about −7°C. Summer is warm, dry and sunny, with afternoons near 28°C and cool nights near 10°C. About 420 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in May and June. Wildfire smoke collects in the same bowl in August and September, and it is the main risk." },
  "moab-ut": { original: "Moab sits at 1245 m in a canyon carved by the Colorado River, walled by red-rock cliffs that radiate heat. Summer afternoons regularly top 38°C. But the same canyon walls shelter winter: January averages 8°C, much warmer than the Colorado Plateau above. Spring and fall are the true glory — warm days, cool nights, an unbeatable rock-climbing climate.", expanded: "Moab sits at 1245 m on the floor of a red-rock valley beside the Colorado River, next to Arches and Canyonlands national parks. The cliffs soak up the sun and give it back in the evening, so summer is hot: afternoons average about 36°C and July reaches about 38°C. The same walls shelter the town in winter, when afternoons reach about 8°C and nights fall to about −5°C. Spring and autumn are the comfortable seasons, with warm days and cool nights. Only about 220 mm of precipitation falls in a year, yet a single thunderstorm over the slickrock can send a flash flood through town. Drought and summer heat are the main risks." },
  "mobile-al": { original: "Mobile receives abundant rainfall from Gulf of Mexico moisture streams; sea breezes clash with interior heat to build summer convection. Tropical systems can deliver extreme multi-day rains.", expanded: "Mobile sits at the head of Mobile Bay on the Gulf of Mexico, and it is one of the rainiest cities in the United States, with about 1720 mm a year. In summer the sea breeze meets hot inland air nearly every afternoon and thunderstorms follow; June and July are the wettest months. Summer afternoons average about 32°C and nights about 22°C, with very humid air. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 18°C and a few frosts. Tropical storms and hurricanes can bring days of rain and a storm surge up the bay. Flooding and hurricanes are the main risks, and summer humidity is the everyday cost." },
  "monterrey-mx": { original: "Monterrey mixes high-insolation semi-arid baseline with summer convection that builds against the eastern sierra. Urban expansion amplifies nocturnal heat; industrial air quality interacts with basin geometry. When Gulf moisture taps in, thunderstorms can drop localized hail.", expanded: "Monterrey lies at about 540 m at the foot of the Sierra Madre Oriental, in a valley framed by the saddle-shaped Cerro de la Silla. The lowlands are semi-arid and hot. Summer afternoons average about 36°C and the heat lasts from May to September. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 23°C and nights near 8°C, broken by short, sharp cold fronts from the north. About 480 mm of rain falls in a year, and September brings nearly a third of it, often from tropical storms off the Gulf that flood the dry bed of the Santa Catarina River. Drought empties the reservoirs in other years. The forested slopes of Chipinque, above the city, are much cooler." },
  "morden-mb": { original: "Morden sits in one of Manitoba's warmer growing-season pockets, close to the Pembina Escarpment and the dry western edge of the Red River lowland. Summers can be hot enough for corn, grapes, and long-season gardens; winters are still fully prairie. The microclimate value lies in heat units and sun, not mildness.", expanded: "Morden lies in southern Manitoba at the foot of the Pembina Escarpment, on the western edge of the Red River Valley. It is one of the warmest spots on the Canadian Prairies in summer: afternoons average about 25°C, and corn, apples and even grapes ripen here. A federal research station has bred hardy fruit trees and roses in the town since 1915. Winter is as severe as anywhere on the prairie, with afternoons near −8°C and nights near −18°C. About 540 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in May and June, and the frost-free season is about 140 days. Winter cold, drought and summer hail are the risks." },
  "morelia-mx": { original: "Morelia sits at 1920 m in the Bajío highlands. Temperatures stay within a narrow band year-round: afternoon highs 24–28°C, nights 7–13°C. The climate is defined not by temperature swings but by the rainy season — June through September delivers dramatic afternoon thunderstorms; the rest of the year is bone-dry and brilliantly sunny. The colonial cantera-pink stone architecture glows in that high-altitude light.", expanded: "Morelia lies at 1920 m in a highland valley of Michoacán, a colonial city built of pink stone. Afternoons are warm in every month, from about 26°C in winter to 32°C in May, but nights are cool. Winter nights average about 4°C, which is colder than visitors expect, and light frost can occur. The year turns on the rains. From June to September, storms build in the afternoon and bring most of the year's 650 mm; from November to April the sky is clear and hardly any rain falls. Nights are often more than 16°C cooler than the afternoons. Few houses have heating." },
  "morgantown-wv": { original: "Morgantown’s ridges catch moisture on southwesterly flow ahead of coastal lows; valleys pool cold air and fog on still nights. Snowfall is moderate but elevation within the county varies enough for 200 m to separate rain from heavy snow. Summers are warm and humid — not coastal, not deep South, but persistently sticky. The university town sits at a climate crossroads between mid-Atlantic storms and Ohio Valley convection.", expanded: "Morgantown lies along the Monongahela River in the deeply cut hill country of northern West Virginia. On still nights cold air and fog settle into the valleys while the ridges stay clear. Summer is warm and humid, with afternoons near 28°C and nights near 16°C. Winter afternoons average about 6°C and nights about −4°C. Snow in town is moderate, but the higher country just to the east gets far more, and a climb of 200 m can be the difference between rain and heavy snow. About 1200 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. Skies are often grey in winter. Flash floods in the narrow valleys are the main hazard." },
  "mount-charleston-nv": { original: "The Spring Mountains rise abruptly from the basin floor where subsidence and dryness dominate. Upslope flow brings cloud and occasional thunderstorms; winter storms deliver real snowpack. The inversion that traps smog and heat below also isolates the high elevations as a climatic refuge — a pattern documented in mountain–basin meteorology worldwide.", expanded: "Mount Charleston is a small community at about 2300 m in the Spring Mountains, less than an hour's drive from Las Vegas. The range rises straight from the Mojave Desert to peaks above 3000 m, and its upper slopes carry pine, fir and ancient bristlecone pine. Summer afternoons average about 25°C, about 15°C cooler than the city below. Winter brings about 255 cm of snow, with afternoons near 6°C and nights near −5°C. About 520 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it in winter, with thunderstorms in July and August. Wildfire is the main risk, and flash floods have damaged the canyon roads." },
  "mystic-ct": { original: "Mystic’s climate is paced by water: fog, drizzle, and moderate summer heat. Tropical systems occasionally brush the coast with surge and wind.", expanded: "Mystic sits on a tidal river that opens onto Fishers Island Sound, on the eastern Connecticut coast. The water sets the pace of the seasons. Spring is cool and often foggy, summer afternoons average about 26°C with a sea breeze, and autumn stays mild into October. Winter is milder than inland Connecticut, with afternoons near 5°C and nights near −4°C, and storms often bring rain or a wet mix where the hills get snow. About 1200 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. The village is close to sea level, and storm surge from hurricanes and nor'easters is the main risk; the 1938 hurricane is still the benchmark." },
  "nags-head-nc": { original: "The Outer Banks are a lesson in exposure: sea breezes cap summer heat, but humidity stays high; winter storms can overwash Highway 12. Each town along the strand has micro-differences in dune height and fetch that matter during storms.", expanded: "Nags Head lies on the Outer Banks, a narrow line of barrier islands off North Carolina with the Atlantic on one side and shallow sounds on the other. The sea breeze holds summer afternoons to about 30°C, though the air is humid and nights stay near 22°C. Winter is mild and windy, with afternoons near 12°C and nights near 4°C. About 1420 mm of rain falls in a year, with the most in July and August. Hurricanes and nor'easters are part of life here. They move the dunes, cut new inlets and wash over Highway 12, the only road. Erosion and storm surge are the main risks." },
  "napa-ca": { original: "The valley floor heats by day; cool air drains from the Petaluma Gap and marine layer spills east, wrapping benchlands in fog until mid-morning. Bench elevation differences of only tens of meters reorder frost risk and hang time — the entire AVA system is a map of microclimate.", expanded: "The city of Napa lies at the southern end of the Napa Valley, between the Mayacamas Mountains and the Vaca Range. Summer days are hot and dry, with afternoons near 30°C. In the evening cool air and fog move up the valley from San Pablo Bay, nights fall to about 13°C, and the fog often lasts until mid-morning. That daily swing is what the vineyards depend on. Almost no rain falls from June to September. Winter is mild and wet, with afternoons near 15°C, and brings most of the year's 700 mm. Wildfire is the serious risk: fires in 2017 and 2020 burned into the hills on both sides of the valley." },
  "naples-fl": { original: "Naples trades Atlantic surf for Gulf shallows: water temperatures seasonally warm, supporting humid tropical-feeling nights. Cold fronts are brief; sea breezes organize summer convection inland.", expanded: "Naples lies on the Gulf coast of southwest Florida, on low sandy ground between the beach and the mangrove estuaries at the edge of the Everglades. Winter is the season people come for: dry and sunny, with afternoons near 25°C and nights near 14°C. Summer is hot and humid, with afternoons near 33°C, nights near 24°C and a thunderstorm on most afternoons. About 1480 mm of rain falls in a year, nearly all of it from June to September. The city stands only a metre or two above the sea, and hurricane storm surge is the major risk. Hurricane Ian flooded much of it in 2022." },
  "nelson-bc": { original: "Nelson perches on the north arm of Kootenay Lake, whose 150-km length and 150-m depth provides substantial thermal moderation to the surrounding steep mountain town. Winters are milder and summers cooler than you'd expect at 49° latitude and 535 m elevation. The arts and counter-culture character traces partly to the mild mountain climate — livable year-round in a way that many Rocky Mountain interior towns are not.", expanded: "Nelson climbs a steep hillside above the West Arm of Kootenay Lake, in the Selkirk Mountains of southeastern British Columbia. The long, deep lake rarely freezes and takes the edge off both seasons. Winter afternoons average about 2°C and nights about −4°C, mild for the interior, and about 250 cm of snow falls in town, with far more on the mountains above. Summer afternoons reach about 27°C and nights cool to about 11°C. About 830 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it from November to January, and the forest is cedar and hemlock. Winter skies are often grey. Wildfire smoke is the main summer risk." },
  "new-orleans-la": { original: "New Orleans is a climate of extremes in humidity: summer heat indices routinely exceed 40°C equivalent; thunderstorms pop on summer afternoons; winter cold snaps are short but can freeze citrus on the North Shore. Hurricane risk is existential to infrastructure.", expanded: "New Orleans lies between the Mississippi River and Lake Pontchartrain, on delta ground that is at or below sea level across much of the city. Summer is long and very humid. Afternoons average about 33°C and nights about 24°C from June to August, and a thunderstorm builds on most afternoons. Winter is short and mild, with afternoons near 18°C and an occasional freeze. About 1740 mm of rain falls in a year. Every drop has to be pumped out, so a heavy downpour can flood streets within an hour. Hurricanes are the major risk. The levees were rebuilt after Katrina in 2005, and the wetlands that once buffered the city keep shrinking." },
  "niagara-on-the-lake": { original: "The Niagara Peninsula is a textbook case of lake moderation plus orographic shelter. Lake Ontario moderates temperature in both directions; the Niagara Escarpment shelters the narrow band of fruit country below from cold air drainage off the Canadian Shield. Winters are mild by Ontario standards; summers are warm and humid; the grape and tender-fruit industries flourish.", expanded: "Niagara-on-the-Lake sits on the narrow plain between Lake Ontario and the Niagara Escarpment. The lake keeps spring cool, which holds the fruit buds back until the frosts are over, and it stays warm through autumn, which delays the first freeze. Air moving between the lake and the escarpment keeps cold from settling on still nights. The frost-free season is about 198 days. Summer afternoons average about 26°C and winter nights about −5°C, mild for Ontario, with about 140 cm of snow. About 810 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. Peaches, cherries and wine grapes are grown here, and a hard freeze each winter is used to make icewine." },
  "norfolk-ct": { original: "Norfolk collects cold air on clear nights the way a bowl collects water. Lake-effect streamers from Lake Ontario occasionally reach this far southeast when flow aligns; more often it is simply elevation and rural exposure. Summers are mild and short; the town is a cultural bridge between New England ski country and the Hudson Valley.", expanded: "Norfolk sits at about 430 m in the hills of northwest Connecticut and calls itself the Icebox of Connecticut. It is higher than nearly every other town in the state, and on clear nights cold air settles into its hollows. Winter afternoons hover just above freezing and nights average about −9°C, with about 230 cm of snow, more than double what falls on the coast. Summer is mild, with afternoons near 25°C and cool nights, which has drawn summer residents and a chamber music festival for more than a century. About 1470 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Spring is late and the growing season is short." },
  "oakland-md": { original: "Oakland is the anchor of Garrett County, where Pittsburgh television markets and lake-effect snow belts overlap. Deep Creek Lake adds local moisture and modifies breezes; ridges catch upslope snow on northwesterly flow. Summer is mild and prized by Mid-Atlantic escapees; spring is late and fickle for growers.", expanded: "Oakland sits at about 750 m on the Allegheny Plateau in Garrett County, the highest and coldest part of Maryland. Northwest winds that have crossed the Great Lakes are forced up the plateau and drop their snow here, about 230 cm in an average winter. Winter afternoons average about 3°C and nights about −7°C. Summer is mild, with afternoons near 25°C and nights near 14°C, and Deep Creek Lake nearby draws visitors from Washington and Baltimore. About 1410 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Spring comes late, and frost can catch gardens in May. Winter driving and heating costs are the things to weigh." },
  "ocean-springs-ms": { original: "Ocean Springs inherits the same humid subtropical core as the Deep South, but the Sound’s daily heat capacity knocks a few degrees off afternoon peaks relative to inland Hattiesburg. Summer nights stay warm; winter cold fronts are brief and often moderated by returning onshore flow. It is not a dry climate — it is a maritime one.", expanded: "Ocean Springs sits under live oaks on the Mississippi Sound, across the bay from Biloxi. The shallow water takes a little heat off summer afternoons, which average about 32°C, but nights stay warm and humid at about 23°C. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 18°C and nights near 7°C; cold fronts pass quickly and frost is light. About 1750 mm of rain falls in a year, with the most in June and July, and thunderstorms build on most summer afternoons. The serious risk is hurricane storm surge. Katrina's surge in 2005 destroyed much of the waterfront here, and insurance costs reflect it." },
  "ojai-ca": { original: "Ojai's east-west orientation gives it a last-light glow found almost nowhere else in Southern California — and a climate shaped by its amphitheater walls. Warm, dry summers and mild winters encourage citrus and olives. Afternoons can be hot inland, but the valley orientation channels cooler sea-breeze intrusions most evenings.", expanded: "Ojai lies in a small valley that runs east to west behind the coastal mountains of Ventura County, with the Topatopa bluffs at its eastern end. At sunset the bluffs turn pink for a few minutes, which locals call the pink moment. Summer days are hot and dry, with afternoons near 30°C and often higher, and evenings cool to about 14°C as sea air works up the valley. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 19°C and occasional frost on the valley floor. About 510 mm of rain falls in a year, nearly all from December to March. Citrus and olives grow here. Wildfire is the serious risk: the Thomas Fire burned around the valley in 2017." },
  "orizaba-mx": { original: "Orizaba sits on the wet side of the Mexican highlands, below the country's highest peak. Gulf moisture rises into the mountains, producing frequent cloud, heavy wet-season rain, and mild temperatures that feel greener and more humid than the drier central plateau. It is a bridge between tropical Veracruz and the cool volcanic highlands.", expanded: "Orizaba lies at about 1230 m in a valley on the wet eastern side of the Mexican highlands, below Pico de Orizaba, the country's highest mountain. Moist air from the Gulf of Mexico rises against the sierra here, so cloud and drizzle are common and the hills are green all year. Temperatures are mild: afternoons run from about 25°C in winter to 30°C in May, and nights from about 10°C to 15°C. About 1410 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from June to September. In winter, cold fronts from the north bring days of mist and light rain. Floods and landslides after heavy rain are the main hazards." },
  "paducah-ky": { original: "Paducah’s climate is classically mid-South: hot, humid summers with afternoon thunderstorms; mild winters punctuated by Arctic outbreaks and freezing rain when warm Gulf air overrides cold domes at the surface. The Ohio and Tennessee rivers moderate temperatures slightly and provide moisture for fog banks in spring and fall. Convective available potential energy is high in June ahead of mesoscale convective systems that track along the river valleys.", expanded: "Paducah stands where the Tennessee River joins the Ohio, on low ground behind a floodwall built after the great flood of 1937. Summer is hot and humid, with afternoons near 31°C, nights near 20°C and frequent thunderstorms. Winter is mild on average, with afternoons near 9°C, but Arctic air reaches here several times a season, and when warm Gulf air rides over it the result is freezing rain. About 1400 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in April and May. River fog is common in spring and autumn. Severe storms and tornadoes, ice storms and river flooding are the main risks." },
  "page-az": { original: "Page is a high-desert hub with July–August thunderstorms that paint ephemeral waterfalls on Navajo sandstone. Winters are mild and sunny; shoulder seasons are ideal for canyoneering. Lake Powell moderates immediate shoreline microclimates but does not erase the arid core.", expanded: "Page sits at about 1310 m on a mesa above Glen Canyon Dam and Lake Powell, in bare sandstone country on the Colorado Plateau. Only about 170 mm of rain falls in a year. Summer is hot and dry, with afternoons near 35°C and nights near 20°C, and from July to September thunderstorms send sudden floods through the slot canyons. Winter is sunny and cool, with afternoons near 9°C and nights just below freezing. Spring and autumn are the comfortable seasons. The lake cools its own shoreline a little and no more. Drought has lowered Lake Powell a long way, and flash floods are the hazard for anyone in a canyon." },
  "palenque-mx": { original: "Palenque sits at the jungle edge: mornings can be misty; afternoons turn steamy with thunder. Rainfall is abundant year-round with a drier winter window favored by tourists. Elevation is low enough that heat never fully disappears.", expanded: "Palenque lies at about 150 m at the foot of the Chiapas highlands, where the Gulf coastal plain meets rainforest-covered hills and the ruins of the Maya city. It is hot and humid all year. Afternoons average about 29°C in winter and reach about 35°C in May, and nights stay at 18–22°C. About 1800 mm of rain falls in a year. No month is dry, but March and April bring the least and September, with about 330 mm, the most. Mornings are often misty and afternoons end in thunder. The cooler, drier months from December to February are the easiest for visitors. Heat and humidity are the things to weigh." },
  "palm-springs-ca": { original: "Palm Springs sits in the venturi of the San Gorgonio Pass where pressure gradients translate into strong winds; winter attracts snowbirds for reliable warmth; summer pushes human activity into pools and evenings. Palm oases exploit groundwater; the surrounding desert is hyperarid.", expanded: "Palm Springs sits at the foot of Mount San Jacinto, which rises nearly 3200 m above the desert floor and blocks almost all rain from the coast. About 150 mm falls in a year. Winter is the season to be here, with sunny afternoons near 22°C and nights near 9°C. Summer afternoons average about 41°C, July reaches about 43°C, and nights stay near 25°C. Wind pours through San Gorgonio Pass just to the north, where thousands of turbines stand. An aerial tramway climbs from the desert floor to cool pine forest near the top. The palm oases and the golf courses both draw on groundwater. Extreme heat and drought are the main risks." },
  "penticton-bc": { original: "Penticton sits between two lakes in one of Canada's driest valleys. Deep Pacific rain-shadow produces semi-arid summer conditions; Okanagan Lake's thermal mass moderates winter, allowing tender fruit and viable wine grapes at 49° latitude. Ponderosa pines and sagebrush fill the hillsides — a Canadian landscape with genuinely American-Southwest character. The two big lakes are the difference between living here and living in similar-latitude prairie cold.", expanded: "Penticton lies on a strip of land between Okanagan Lake and Skaha Lake, in one of the driest valleys in Canada. The Coast and Cascade ranges take most of the Pacific rain, and only about 340 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Summer is hot and sunny, with afternoons near 28°C and nights near 13°C. The two lakes soften winter, when afternoons average about 3°C, nights about −3°C and only about 55 cm of snow falls. That mildness is what allows peaches, apricots and wine grapes at 49° north. The hillsides are ponderosa pine and sagebrush. Wildfire and smoke are the serious summer risks." },
  "pincher-creek-ab": { original: "Pincher Creek sits directly east of Crowsnest Pass, one of the strongest chinook outflow channels in North America. Winters here are wild, windy, and intermittent rather than continuous: two-day thaws to 15°C separate weeks of prairie cold. Wind gusts of 120+ km/h are recorded most winters. The landscape (fescue prairie, cottonwood riparian) reflects the chinook regime — and so do Alberta's world-leading wind-energy installations nearby.", expanded: "Pincher Creek sits at 1190 m in the Alberta foothills, directly east of Crowsnest Pass. Westerly winds funnel through the pass and come down onto the prairie as chinooks. In winter a chinook can lift the temperature to 15°C for a day or two between spells of deep cold, and gusts above 120 km/h are recorded in most years. Winter afternoons average around freezing and nights about −10°C, with about 200 cm of snow that seldom stays long. Summer is cool, with afternoons near 24°C. About 480 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in June. Wind farms line the ridges. The wind itself is what residents must make peace with." },
  "pittsfield-ma": { original: "Pittsfield sits in a valley network that channels winter cold and summer thunderstorms along frontal boundaries. It is not as harsh as Vermont’s Northeast Kingdom, but it is dramatically different from Cape Cod on the same state license plate. Fall color and spring mud season are serious social calendars.", expanded: "Pittsfield lies at about 320 m in the Housatonic valley of the Berkshires, between the Taconic Range and the Berkshire plateau. It is well inland and well above sea level, so winter is colder and snowier than in eastern Massachusetts. Afternoons average about 1°C and nights about −9°C, and about 190 cm of snow falls. Summer is mild, with afternoons near 26°C and cool nights near 13°C. About 1280 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in June and July, when thunderstorms follow the valleys. Autumn colour peaks in early October. Spring is slow and muddy, and the frost-free season is about five months." },
  "point-reyes-ca": { original: "Point Reyes juts 15 km into the Pacific at the Bay Area's north edge and catches the full force of California Current upwelling. The outer lighthouse recorded the highest fog frequency in the continental US. Step inland 8 km and you gain 8°C on a typical summer afternoon. The dairy ranches thriving here for 150 years are the direct consequence of the unchanging cool.", expanded: "The Point Reyes Peninsula reaches about 15 km into the Pacific north of San Francisco, into cold water that wells up along the coast. The outer headland and its lighthouse are among the foggiest and windiest places on the Pacific coast, and summer there stays cold. The figures in this profile are for Point Reyes Station, the village on the sheltered inland side. Summer afternoons there average about 24°C once the fog lifts, and nights fall to about 11°C. Winter is mild and wet, with afternoons near 15°C, and brings nearly all of the year's 830 mm of rain. Dairy ranches have worked these cool pastures since the 1850s." },
  "prescott-az": { original: "Prescott's 1636 m elevation earns it Ponderosa pine forest and a genuine four-season climate by Arizona standards. Summer highs hold in the 30°C range; winter brings occasional snow and freezing nights. It's one of Arizona's great summer climate refuges — heat-sensitive desert-dwellers move up for the warm months. The monsoon is reliable; sunset storms build every afternoon in July and August.", expanded: "Prescott sits at 1636 m among granite hills and ponderosa pine in central Arizona, about 1300 m above Phoenix. That height makes summer afternoons about 10°C cooler than in the desert. They average about 31°C, and nights fall to about 15°C. From July to September, thunderstorms build most afternoons and bring about 40% of the year's 460 mm of precipitation. Winter is sunny, with afternoons near 12°C, nights just below freezing and a few snowfalls that melt within days. May and June are hot, dry and windy, and that is the fire season. Wildfire, smoke and a limited groundwater supply are the main risks." },
  "prince-edward-co-on": { original: "A near-island jutting into Lake Ontario, PEC enjoys measurable lake moderation and sits on a limestone plateau that drains fast and warms quickly in spring. Cool-climate wine — Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling — has flourished here since 2000.", expanded: "Prince Edward County is a limestone peninsula, nearly an island, reaching into the eastern end of Lake Ontario. Water on almost every side delays spring and lengthens autumn, and the frost-free season is about 180 days. Summer afternoons average about 25°C and nights about 15°C. The thin soil over limestone drains fast and warms early. Winter is cold, with afternoons around freezing and nights near −8°C, cold enough that growers bury their grapevines in soil each autumn to protect them. About 970 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year, with about 140 cm of snow. Pinot Noir and Chardonnay are the main wine grapes, and summer drought on shallow soil is the growers' main worry." },
  "prince-rupert-bc": { original: "Prince Rupert sits at the mouth of the Skeena River, where Pacific storms slam into the Coast Mountains. Annual precipitation runs well over 2,000 mm (the airport gauge has averaged 2,300–2,600 mm); days of sunshine are few. Winter lows rarely drop below freezing; summer highs rarely exceed 18°C. Fog, drizzle, and low cloud define the sensory character. The historic cannery town of the North Coast is hyper-maritime and truly distinct within Canada.", expanded: "Prince Rupert sits on Kaien Island near the mouth of the Skeena River, where Pacific storms meet the Coast Mountains. It is one of the wettest and cloudiest cities in Canada. Well over 2000 mm of precipitation falls in a year, rain comes in every month, and October and November are the wettest. The ocean keeps the temperature in a narrow range: winter afternoons reach about 6°C and nights hover around freezing, while summer afternoons reach only about 16°C. Snow is light and soon washed away. Rainforest of cedar, hemlock and spruce comes down to the water. People who need sun find the grey months hard." },
  "puebla-mx": { original: "Puebla blends highland spring climate with serious urban pollution in winter inversions. Rain is concentrated summer–monsoon; dry season skies are cobalt. Volcanic ash soils support floriculture and maize.", expanded: "Puebla lies at 2150 m in a high valley east of the volcanoes Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl. Altitude keeps it mild. Afternoons run from about 24°C in winter to 29°C in May, and winter nights fall to about 6°C. Rain comes from June to September, mostly in afternoon storms, and brings most of the year's 810 mm. The dry season is sunny, with deep blue skies. In winter, still air can trap haze over the city in the morning. Popocatépetl is active and dusts the valley with ash from time to time, which closes the airport and calls for face masks. The same ash has made the soils fertile." },
  "puerto-escondido-mx": { original: "Puerto Escondido is the Pacific-coast capital of Mexican tropical-dry climate. The annual temperature range is narrow — highs 29–33°C, lows 18–24°C — but rainfall is highly seasonal. November through April is bone-dry, brown-landscape weather; May through October is a green explosion fed by mesoscale convective systems and occasional hurricanes. The famous surf break is partly a product of the offshore bathymetry but also of the steady trade-wind regime.", expanded: "Puerto Escondido lies on the Pacific coast of Oaxaca, backed by the Sierra Madre del Sur. Temperature hardly changes through the year: afternoons stay at 33–35°C and nights range from about 18°C in January to 22°C in summer. Rain is what changes. From November to April almost none falls, and the hills turn brown and leafless. From May to October about 970 mm falls, the forest turns green, and the air is very humid. Hurricanes form offshore in those months, and Hurricane Agatha came ashore just down the coast in 2022. The surf at Zicatela beach is powerful and has dangerous currents." },
  "puerto-vallarta-mx": { original: "Puerto Vallarta sits where bay curvature and sierra slopes focus afternoon convection. Winter is the dry, golden high season; summer brings muggy heat and Pacific storms. Sea breezes keep beaches cooler than inland Guadalajara by a wide margin.", expanded: "Puerto Vallarta lies on the Bay of Banderas, with the forested Sierra Madre rising directly behind the city. Winter is dry and sunny, with afternoons near 30°C and nights near 16°C, and that is the busy season. Summer is hot and very humid, with afternoons near 34°C and nights near 23°C. About three-quarters of the year's 1070 mm of rain falls from July to September, usually in heavy evening storms that come down off the mountains. Hurricanes pass offshore in most years and sometimes strike, as Kenna did in 2002. Flooding rivers and landslides on the steep slopes are the other hazards." },
  "queretaro-mx": { original: "Querétaro is a comfort-climate compromise: warmer and drier than Mexico City, less humid than lowland tropics, and increasingly urban. The dry season brings clear light and large day-night swings; the wet season arrives through afternoon storms that break heat but strain drainage in expanding districts.", expanded: "Querétaro lies at 1820 m on the edge of the Bajío, the high plain of central Mexico. It is warmer and drier than Mexico City. Afternoons run from about 24°C in winter to 30°C in May, and winter nights fall to about 7°C. Only about 480 mm of rain falls in a year, nearly all from June to September in afternoon storms that can briefly flood streets in the newer districts. The dry season is long and sunny, with cool mornings. The city has grown fast as an industrial centre, and its water is the long-term concern: the aquifer is overdrawn and supply is piped in from far away." },
  "rapid-city-sd": { original: "Rapid City sits where Great Plains simplicity meets orographic complexity; winter can flip from arctic to spring-like in hours when downslope winds arrive.", expanded: "Rapid City lies at about 970 m where the Great Plains meet the eastern edge of the Black Hills. Weather changes quickly here. In winter, air coming down off the hills can lift the temperature from deep cold to well above freezing in an hour or two, and the area is known locally as the banana belt of South Dakota. Winter afternoons average about 4°C and nights about −9°C. Summer afternoons average about 28°C, with cool nights. About 490 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in May and June, often in thunderstorms with hail. A flash flood on Rapid Creek killed 238 people in 1972, and the floodplain is now parkland." },
  "real-catorce-mx": { original: "Real de Catorce sits at 2756 m on a mountain above a vast Chihuahuan steppe. The old silver-mining town was largely abandoned after ore played out; elevation and aridity have preserved the ruins. The climate is cool year-round (highs 20°C, lows near freezing in winter), with minimal precipitation and crystal-clear skies. Access is via a 2.3-km historic tunnel drilled through the mountain — one of Mexico's most memorable arrivals.", expanded: "Real de Catorce sits at 2756 m in the Sierra de Catorce, high above the Chihuahuan Desert of San Luis Potosí. The only road in passes through the Ogarrio Tunnel, 2.3 km long, cut for the silver mines. The town emptied when the mines closed and has been partly resettled. Altitude keeps it cool: afternoons reach about 17°C in winter and about 25°C in May, and winter nights fall close to freezing. Only about 380 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from June to September. The air is thin and dry and the sky very clear. The desert below is Wirikuta, sacred land of the Wixárika people. Water is scarce." },
  "revelstoke-bc": { original: "Revelstoke lies on the Columbia River directly between the Selkirks and Monashees. Pacific moisture that hasn't already rained out on the Coast Range is wrung out here by successive orographic barriers — producing some of the continent's heaviest snowfall zones. The town receives about 4.5 m of annual snowfall at valley level; the ski area on Mount Mackenzie averages 10 m. Summers are mild and green.", expanded: "Revelstoke lies on the Columbia River between the Monashee and Selkirk mountains. Pacific air that has already crossed the Coast Mountains still carries plenty of moisture, and the Selkirks force it up again. The town gets about 600 cm of snow in an average winter and the mountains above it get far more. Winter is not especially cold, with afternoons around freezing and nights near −6°C, but the sky is grey for weeks. Summer is green and mild, with afternoons near 25°C and cool nights. About 870 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Avalanches close the highway through Rogers Pass in most winters, and heavy snow on the roof is a routine chore." },
  "roswell-nm": { original: "Roswell sits in a wide valley where dryline and monsoon moisture meet; spring wind and dust are notorious. Irrigation from the Pecos supports pockets of agriculture.", expanded: "Roswell lies at 1087 m in the broad valley of the Pecos River, on the dry plains of southeastern New Mexico. Summer is hot and sunny, with afternoons near 35°C and nights near 18°C. From July to September, scattered thunderstorms bring much of the year's 310 mm of rain. Winter days are mild and bright, near 15°C, and nights fall to about −3°C. Spring is the hard season: strong west winds blow dust across the valley for days. Farms here grow alfalfa and pecans on water from an artesian aquifer that has been pumped for more than a century. Drought and the state of that aquifer are the long-term concerns." },
  "salt-spring-bc": { original: "The Southern Gulf Islands get a fraction of the outer coast's rain — the Olympic and Vancouver Island rain shadows reach this far north and east. Salt Spring's sheltered bays and Garry oak meadows reveal a climate more Mediterranean than most of coastal BC.", expanded: "Salt Spring is the largest of the southern Gulf Islands, in the sheltered water between Vancouver Island and the mainland. The mountains of Vancouver Island and the Olympics take the worst of the Pacific storms, and the island gets about 1290 mm of rain a year, less than half of what falls on the outer coast. Nearly all of it comes from October to March. Summer is dry and mild, with afternoons near 22°C. Winter is grey and gentle, with afternoons near 6°C and little frost. Garry oak meadows and arbutus grow on the dry rocky slopes. Fresh water runs short in late summer, and everything arrives by ferry." },
  "saltillo-mx": { original: "Saltillo sits at 1600 m in the Chihuahuan high desert, only 85 km west of Monterrey — but the elevation delivers an 8°C cooler summer afternoon on average. Winters are clear, dry, and can produce occasional frost. The altitude-tempered climate shaped the colonial founding; the city's historic position as the Camino Real's northern hub reflects the rare oasis of livable climate in a vast dry region.", expanded: "Saltillo lies at 1600 m in a high desert valley behind the Sierra Madre Oriental, about 85 km west of Monterrey and more than 1000 m above it. That height makes summer afternoons about 6°C cooler than in Monterrey; they average about 30°C, with nights near 15°C. Winter is dry and clear, with afternoons near 20°C and nights near 5°C, and cold fronts from the north bring frost and, now and then, a dusting of snow. Only about 340 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from June to September. The air is dry in every season. Water supply is the long-term concern for a fast-growing industrial city." },
  "santa-cruz-felton-ca": { original: "The San Lorenzo Valley carves through coast-redwood forest above Santa Cruz. Summer marine layer pours up the valley each evening, feeding the tallest trees on Earth. Walk fifteen minutes from a sunny canyon bench into a fog-dripping redwood cove and the temperature drops 5°C.", expanded: "Felton lies in the San Lorenzo Valley, among coast redwoods in the Santa Cruz Mountains, about 10 km inland from Monterey Bay. On summer evenings fog moves up the valley from the bay. Nights are cool, about 10°C, and the redwoods collect fog on their needles and drip it to the ground. Once the fog burns off, the sheltered valley heats up, and afternoons average about 30°C. Winter is wet: about 1060 mm of rain falls in a year, nearly all from November to March, and the river can flood. Summer is rainless. Wildfire is the serious risk. The CZU Lightning Complex fire burned through the mountains just to the north in 2020." },
  "savannah-ga": { original: "Savannah’s live-oak canopy and river–ocean geometry soften peak heat compared to inland Georgia; tropical cyclones and spring severe weather still define hazard calendars.", expanded: "Savannah sits on a low bluff above the Savannah River, about 30 km inland from the Atlantic, laid out around shaded squares under live oaks. Summer is long, hot and humid, with afternoons near 33°C, nights near 23°C and a thunderstorm on many afternoons. July and August are the wettest months of a year that totals about 1270 mm. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 18°C and a few frosts. Spring comes early, with azaleas in March. The marshes and tidal creeks around the city are barely above sea level. Storm surge and flooding from hurricanes are the main risks, and high tides already reach some roads." },
  "scottsbluff-ne": { original: "Scottsbluff sits where irrigation from the North Platte meets semi-arid steppe. Summer brings dryline thunderstorms; winter alternates between bitter cold and chinook relief.", expanded: "Scottsbluff lies at about 1190 m in the North Platte valley of western Nebraska, below the sandstone bluff that guided travellers on the Oregon Trail. This is the dry High Plains. Only about 380 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it from April to July, and crops such as sugar beets and beans depend on irrigation from the river. Summer afternoons average about 31°C, but the dry air lets nights cool to about 14°C. Winter swings between Arctic cold and mild, windy spells when air comes down off the Rockies; afternoons average about 6°C and nights about −9°C. Hail and drought are the main risks." },
  "spearfish-sd": { original: "Spearfish sits at a canyon mouth where stable westerly flow can descend and warm adiabatically, replacing dense cold air in the valley with dramatic speed. Such events are studied in synoptic meteorology courses because they are so readable on surface traces. Summer is warm and stormy on the Plains edge; winter is a story of wind and rapid change rather than monolithic cold.", expanded: "Spearfish sits at about 1110 m at the mouth of Spearfish Canyon, on the northern edge of the Black Hills. It holds a world record: on 22 January 1943 a chinook raised the temperature by 27°C in two minutes. Such warm winds, coming down off the hills, break up the winter many times a season. Winter afternoons average about 4°C and nights about −9°C. The hills also wring out moisture, and the town gets about 610 mm of precipitation and about 245 cm of snow a year, far more than the plains to the north. Summer afternoons average about 29°C, with thunderstorms. Wildfire and drought are the main risks." },
  "spokane-wa": { original: "Spokane sits where the plateau opens east of the Cascades: roughly half Seattle’s annual precipitation but real winter cold and reliable summer sun. Pacific moisture arrives in modified form; Arctic outbreaks still punch through. The river corridor organizes urban heat island and nocturnal cold drainage in the older valley neighborhoods.", expanded: "Spokane lies at about 560 m on the Spokane River in eastern Washington, where the dry Columbia Basin meets the pine-covered foothills of the Rockies. The Cascades take most of the Pacific rain, and the city gets about 440 mm of precipitation a year, roughly half of Seattle's. Summer is dry and sunny, with afternoons near 29°C and cool nights near 12°C. Winter is cold and often grey, with afternoons near 4°C, nights near −3°C and about 135 cm of snow. Most of the year's moisture arrives between November and January. Wildfire smoke in August and September is the main drawback." },
  "squamish-bc": { original: "Squamish is where the Sea-to-Sky Highway meets the ocean: summer is warm by Vancouver standards but still maritime; winter brings heavy rain and occasional valley snow when Arctic air damps against the Strait. The eponymous wind races south when pressure gradients align — a celebrated kite- and wing-surfing engine.", expanded: "Squamish lies at the head of Howe Sound, a fjord north of Vancouver, beneath the granite wall of the Stawamus Chief. Mountains on both sides wring out Pacific storms, and about 2240 mm of precipitation falls in a year, most of it from October to March. Winter is mild at sea level, with afternoons near 6°C, though Arctic air sometimes pours down the valley from the interior as a strong, cold north wind. On summer afternoons the wind blows the other way, up the sound, steady enough to make the town a centre for kiteboarding. Summer afternoons reach about 24°C. Floods, landslides and debris flows on steep ground are the main hazards." },
  "st-johns-nl": { original: "St. John's holds the distinction of being Canada's foggiest, cloudiest, wettest, windiest, and snowiest major city all at once — a consequence of its position on the Avalon Peninsula at the boundary of the cold Labrador Current and the North Atlantic storm track. Summers are cool (highs around 20°C); winters mild by Canadian standards but relentlessly stormy. Annual sunshine is among the lowest on the continent.", expanded: "St. John's sits on a small rock-walled harbour on the Avalon Peninsula of Newfoundland, the easternmost city in North America. The cold Labrador Current runs just offshore and the North Atlantic storm track passes overhead. The result is a city that ranks among Canada's foggiest, windiest and cloudiest. Summer is cool, with afternoons near 19°C. Winter is mild for Canada, with afternoons near 1°C and nights near −5°C, but stormy, and about 355 cm of snow falls. A single blizzard in January 2020 left about 75 cm. About 1380 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Icebergs drift past in spring." },
  "state-college-pa": { original: "State College sits in a valley between long parallel ridges; radiative nights run cold; spring green-up can lag neighboring ridges. Winter storms pick up moisture from the Great Lakes in northwesterly flow.", expanded: "State College lies at about 360 m in the Nittany Valley of central Pennsylvania, between long parallel ridges. On clear nights cold air settles on the valley floor, so the valley runs colder than its elevation suggests and spring arrives a little late. Summer is warm and humid, with afternoons near 27°C and nights near 16°C. Winter afternoons average about 3°C and nights about −5°C. Northwest winds bring snow showers from the Great Lakes, though most of that snow falls on the higher plateau to the west. About 1100 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. The valley has four clear seasons and few extremes." },
  "summerland-bc": { original: "Summerland sits mid-Okanagan on benches above the lake. Lake moderation buffers winter; rain-shadow dryness and long summer sun power one of Canada's finest orchard-vineyard climates.", expanded: "Summerland sits on terraces above the west shore of Okanagan Lake, about midway along the valley. The Coast and Cascade ranges take most of the Pacific rain, and only about 370 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Summer is hot and sunny, with afternoons near 27°C and nights near 13°C. The deep lake softens winter, when afternoons average about 2°C and nights about −4°C, with about 70 cm of snow. Orchards and vineyards cover the benches, where cold air drains away toward the lake on spring nights. A federal research station here has studied fruit growing since 1914. Wildfire, smoke and drought are the main risks." },
  "sunshine-coast-bc": { original: "The Sunshine Coast sits partially in the lee of Vancouver Island's spine, receiving noticeably more sun and less rain than the nearby mainland and the outer island coast. Mild winters, warm-ish summers by Pacific standards, and reliable shoulder seasons.", expanded: "The Sunshine Coast is the stretch of mainland shore northwest of Vancouver, around Gibsons and Sechelt, reached only by ferry. The mountains of Vancouver Island take some of the rain before storms cross the Strait of Georgia, and the name, coined to sell land, is fair in summer. July and August are dry and mild, with afternoons near 22°C. Winter is grey, wet and gentle, with afternoons near 7°C and nights near 3°C. About 1330 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from October to March. The frost-free season is more than nine months. In recent dry summers the water supply has run short." },
  "sutton-qc": { original: "Sutton occupies the Appalachian side of southern Quebec: wetter and hillier than Montreal, less severe than the deep north, and full of small frost and slope contrasts. Valley floors cool quickly on clear nights, while south-facing slopes and village benches carry orchards, vineyards, and maple woods.", expanded: "Sutton lies at the foot of the Sutton Mountains in Quebec's Eastern Townships, a few kilometres from the Vermont border. The Appalachian hills make it wetter and snowier than Montreal: about 1280 mm of precipitation falls in a year, including about 250 cm of snow. Winter is cold, with afternoons near −2°C and nights near −12°C. Summer is warm and green, with afternoons near 25°C. On clear nights the valley floors cool quickly and catch frost, while the slopes above stay milder, and that is where the orchards, vineyards and maple woods are. The frost-free season is about 150 days. Winter cold is the main thing to weigh." },
  "syracuse-ny": { original: "Syracuse sits 50 km south of Lake Ontario, directly in the line of fire for the lake-effect snow machine. The city's Golden Snowball award tracks its position as America's snowiest major metropolitan area most years. Summer is humid and mild; the lake moderates extreme cold and heat compared to cities further from water.", expanded: "Syracuse lies about 55 km southeast of Lake Ontario, in the path of the northwest winds that cross the lake in winter. Those winds bring about 380 cm of snow in an average year, which makes it the snowiest large city in the United States in most winters. The snow comes in bursts, a few intense days at a time, and winter skies are grey between them. Winter afternoons average about 2°C and nights about −7°C. Summer is warm and humid, with afternoons near 27°C. About 1090 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. The city is well equipped for snow, and the roads usually reopen quickly." },
  "taos-nm": { original: "Taos sits on a basalt plateau below the southern Sangre de Cristos. The elevation produces dramatic diurnal swings (summer highs near 27–29°C, lows in the single digits), sharp winter cold interrupted by bright sun, and a monsoon regime that reliably delivers dramatic summer thunderstorms. The light is famously extraordinary — an atmosphere-of-air quality that brought painters here for a century.", expanded: "Taos sits at 2124 m on a high plateau between the Sangre de Cristo Mountains and the gorge of the Rio Grande. The air is thin, dry and very clear. Summer afternoons reach about 28°C and nights fall to about 9°C. Winter is sunny and cold, with afternoons near 7°C, nights near −9°C and about 120 cm of snow in town. Thunderstorms in July and August bring the largest share of the year's 360 mm of precipitation. Painters have come for the light since 1898, and Taos Pueblo has been lived in for about a thousand years. Wildfire, smoke and drought are the main risks." },
  "tapalpa-mx": { original: "Tapalpa sits at 2020 m on a volcanic plateau in western Jalisco. Cool pine forest surrounds the town; summer afternoons rarely exceed 24°C.", expanded: "Tapalpa sits at 2020 m on a forested plateau in the mountains of southern Jalisco, among pine and oak. It is cool for Mexico at any time of year. Winter afternoons reach about 23°C and nights fall to about 5°C, with occasional frost. May is the warmest month, near 29°C. The rains begin in June and last through September, bringing most of the year's 840 mm and cloudy, cool afternoons of about 25°C. Houses are built with fireplaces. The town is a weekend retreat for people from Guadalajara, about two hours away, and the unpaved back roads turn to mud in the wet season." },
  "taxco-mx": { original: "Taxco demonstrates pure lapse-rate magic: tropical latitude but high enough for cool nights and bougainvillea draped over stone streets. Afternoon thunderstorms arrive in summer with theatrical lightning over ridges.", expanded: "Taxco is a silver-mining town built on a steep hillside at about 1770 m in northern Guerrero, with whitewashed houses along lanes too narrow for most cars. Altitude keeps it mild. Afternoons run from about 27°C in winter to 31°C in May, and nights from about 11°C to 15°C. From November to April almost no rain falls. From June to September, thunderstorms arrive most afternoons, often with lightning along the ridges, and bring most of the year's 980 mm. The slopes are steep enough that heavy rain causes landslides and washouts, and that is the main hazard here." },
  "tequila-mx": { original: "The Tequila region sits at 1000–1500 m on the flanks of the Tequila Volcano, with volcanic soils rich in minerals and a climate of warm days, cool nights, and concentrated summer rains. Blue agave thrives in this specific combination, shared at scale only with the Los Altos highlands to the east. The 35,000-hectare agave landscape is a UNESCO World Heritage cultural site precisely because the climate-soil combination is irreplaceable.", expanded: "Tequila lies at about 1180 m in a valley at the foot of the Tequila Volcano in western Jalisco. Fields of blue agave cover the red volcanic soils around it; the landscape, about 35,000 hectares, is a UNESCO World Heritage site. Days are hot for much of the year: afternoons average about 28°C in winter and reach about 35°C in May. Nights are much cooler, about 10°C in winter. About 820 mm of rain falls in a year, half of it in July and August, and winter and spring are dry. Agave takes six years or more to mature and tolerates the long dry season well." },
  "thunder-bay-on": { original: "Thunder Bay sits on Lake Superior's north shore where Great Lakes moderation bumps into true boreal continental climate. The lake delays both winter freeze and spring warmth, keeping lakeshore milder than the Shield just inland. Summers are cool; winters long. Lake-effect snow from Superior is substantial when winds are right. The city's character — boreal forest at the back door, cold freshwater sea at the front — is distinctive.", expanded: "Thunder Bay lies on the northwest shore of Lake Superior, with boreal forest at its back and the long mesa of the Sleeping Giant across the harbour. The lake cools the shore in summer, when afternoons average about 24°C and nights about 10°C, and takes a little off the cold in early winter. Winter is long and severe all the same. Afternoons average about −6°C and nights about −17°C, with about 195 cm of snow. About 680 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in June and July. The frost-free season is about four months. Winter cold is the main thing to weigh, and smoke from northern forest fires reaches the city in some summers." },
  "todos-santos-mx": { original: "Todos Santos sits in a freshwater oasis on the Pacific coast of Baja Sur. The cold Pacific keeps summers several degrees cooler than the Sea of Cortez side. Winters are perfect — warm days, cool nights, sunny.", expanded: "Todos Santos lies on the Pacific side of the Baja California peninsula, almost exactly on the Tropic of Cancer. A spring at the foot of the Sierra de la Laguna feeds palm groves, mango orchards and vegetable fields in the middle of the desert. The Pacific keeps it a few degrees cooler than La Paz on the Gulf side, with summer afternoons near 34°C. Winter is sunny and dry, with afternoons near 27°C and nights near 11°C. Only about 200 mm of rain falls in a year, and half of it comes in September, usually from a tropical storm. Hurricanes and a limited water supply are the main risks." },
  "tofino-bc": { original: "Tofino sits on the exposed outer coast of Vancouver Island, unshielded from Pacific storms. Winter brings some of the most dramatic storm-watching on the continent — 10 m swells, 80-knot winds — and 3300 mm of annual rain. Summers are cool, damp, and fog-prone. Temperature range is tiny: January and July mean highs differ by only ~9°C.", expanded: "Tofino sits on the outer coast of Vancouver Island, fully exposed to the Pacific. About 3050 mm of rain falls in a year, and November, December and January each bring more than 400 mm. Winter storms arrive with heavy surf and gale-force wind, and watching them has become a reason to visit. The ocean keeps the temperature in a narrow range: winter afternoons average about 8°C and summer afternoons only about 18°C. Summer is the drier season, though fog is common in August. Old-growth cedar, hemlock and spruce grow down to the beaches. The town is small, costly to live in and a long drive from a hospital." },
  "tofino-ucluelet-corridor": { original: "Ucluelet shares its climate with Tofino — hyper-maritime, cool-summer, intensely wet. Annual precipitation exceeds 3300 mm; winter storms arrive as Pacific gales. Summer highs rarely exceed 19°C; winter lows rarely below 2°C. The year spans only about 10°C — practically tropical in variability. Storm-watching as tourism emerged here for a reason.", expanded: "Ucluelet lies on a rocky peninsula on the outer coast of Vancouver Island, 40 km south of Tofino, and shares its climate. About 3060 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from October to March, when one Pacific storm follows another. Winter afternoons average about 8°C and nights about 3°C; frost is uncommon. Summer afternoons reach only about 18°C, and fog often sits on the coast in August. The warmest and coldest months are only about 10°C apart. The Wild Pacific Trail follows the shore past the Amphitrite Point lighthouse. Storms, and tsunami exposure on a low open coast, are the hazards." },
  "truckee-ca": { original: "Truckee sits at 1810 m in a Sierra basin where cold air drains from surrounding peaks and pools against the Truckee River valley floor. It is among the coldest reliably-inhabited towns in California, with summer frosts in any month of the year. A 20-minute drive to Tahoe's lakeshore gains 6–10°C of moderation on a winter night — a textbook cold-pool/lake-moderation contrast inside a single watershed.", expanded: "Truckee lies at 1810 m in a basin just east of the Sierra Nevada crest, about 15 km north of Lake Tahoe. On clear nights cold air drains off the surrounding peaks and collects on the basin floor, and on summer mornings the town is often the coldest spot in the contiguous United States. Summer afternoons reach about 27°C and nights fall to about 6°C, with frost possible in any month. Winter afternoons average about 6°C and nights about −8°C. Storms crossing Donner Pass bring about 680 cm of snow in an average winter. The frost-free season is only about 90 days. Wildfire, smoke and heavy snow are the main risks." },
  "tucson-az": { original: "Tucson’s summer rhythm is convection: moisture imported from the Gulf of California meets orographic and urban triggers. Winter brings cool days and brilliant air; hard freezes are rare in the basin floor.", expanded: "Tucson lies at about 730 m in a desert basin ringed by mountain ranges. Summer is very hot, with afternoons near 39°C from June to August. June is the hottest and driest month. In July the monsoon arrives, and afternoon thunderstorms bring about half of the year's 270 mm of rain by the end of September, along with humidity, dust storms and flash floods in the washes. Winter is sunny and mild, with afternoons near 20°C and nights near 4°C. The road up Mount Lemmon climbs from saguaro desert to fir forest in about an hour, into air about 15°C cooler. Extreme heat and a water supply that depends on the Colorado River are the main risks." },
  "valle-guadalupe-mx": { original: "Valle de Guadalupe is a narrow granitic valley 25 km inland from Ensenada, receiving enough marine cooling most mornings to preserve Mediterranean character despite the high latitude for Mexico. Warm sunny summer afternoons give way to cool nights as marine fog creeps up the valley. Wine production — Nebbiolo, Tempranillo, Grenache, and increasingly Mediterranean whites — has made the valley one of Mexico's defining viticultural regions.", expanded: "Valle de Guadalupe lies at about 400 m in the hills of northern Baja California, about 25 km inland from the Pacific. Most of Mexico's wine is made here. Summer days are sunny and hot, near 30°C, but cool sea air and fog move up the valley in the evening and nights fall to about 12°C. Winter is mild, with afternoons near 20°C and nights near 3°C, and brings nearly all of the year's rain, only about 240 mm. Summer is rainless. The vineyards depend on wells, and the aquifer is the valley's limiting resource; water, more than weather, will decide how much more it can grow. Wildfire is the other risk." },
  "viroqua-wi": { original: "The Driftless Area escaped continental glaciation; the result is a countryside with real topography, deep valleys, and ridge-valley microclimate contrasts that are rare elsewhere in the Upper Midwest. Viroqua has become the de facto center of the country's organic and cooperative farming movement — a cultural result of the climate/soil/topography permutation. Spring creeks hold water; ridgetops shed frost; south-facing slopes warm weeks ahead of north faces.", expanded: "Viroqua sits on a ridge at about 395 m in the Driftless Area of southwest Wisconsin, hill country that the glaciers never flattened. Ridges, steep valleys and cold spring-fed creeks give it more varied ground than most of the Midwest. Frost settles in the valleys while the ridge tops stay clear, and south-facing slopes green up well before north-facing ones. Summer afternoons average about 26°C. Winter is cold, with afternoons near −2°C, nights near −12°C and about 110 cm of snow. About 1030 mm of precipitation falls in a year, with the most in June and July. The county has one of the highest concentrations of organic farms in the country. Flash floods in the narrow valleys are the main hazard." },
  "washington-dc": { original: "D.C.’s climate mixes continental and maritime air; the Potomac moderates some edges while concrete elevates nocturnal heat. Summer dew points routinely support heat indices above advisory thresholds.", expanded: "Washington lies on the Potomac River where the Piedmont meets the coastal plain. Summer is hot and humid, with afternoons near 30°C and nights near 19°C, and the built-up centre stays several degrees warmer after dark than the suburbs. Humidity often makes it feel hotter than the thermometer reads. Winter is cool and changeable, with afternoons near 8°C and nights just below freezing, and snow varies greatly from year to year. About 1190 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. Spring and autumn are the comfortable seasons, and the cherry trees bloom around the end of March. Summer heat, severe thunderstorms and flooding along the rivers are the main risks." },
  "wilmington-de": { original: "Wilmington sits in the transition band between maritime Mid-Atlantic and interior heat-pool air; the Brandywine gorge organizes local breezes and cold pools on clear nights.", expanded: "Wilmington lies where Brandywine Creek and the Christina River meet the Delaware, on the line between the rolling Piedmont and the flat coastal plain. Summer is warm and humid, with afternoons near 29°C and nights near 19°C. Winter is cool, with afternoons near 7°C and nights near −2°C, and storms often bring a mix of rain and snow. About 1250 mm of precipitation falls evenly through the year. On clear nights the Brandywine valley north of the city runs colder than the built-up riverfront. Flooding is the main risk. The remains of Hurricane Ida pushed the Brandywine to a record height in 2021 and flooded the city's low-lying neighbourhoods." },
  "winthrop-wa": { original: "The Methow sits in one of the deepest rain shadows in the Pacific Northwest: storms unload on the Cascades to the west, leaving Winthrop with high-desert sunshine and cold, stagnant air in the valley floor during Arctic outbreaks. Cross-country skiing and dark-sky stargazing are world-class; smoke from regional fires is the summer tradeoff.", expanded: "Winthrop lies at about 520 m in the Methow Valley, east of the North Cascades. The mountains take most of the Pacific moisture, and the valley is sunny, with pine on the slopes and sagebrush on the benches. Winter is cold and snowy. Cold air settles on the valley floor, afternoons average around freezing and nights about −8°C, and Washington's all-time record low was measured here and at nearby Mazama in December 1968. The snow supports one of the largest cross-country ski trail networks in North America. Summer is hot and dry, with afternoons near 29°C and cool nights near 10°C. Wildfire and smoke are the serious risks, and large fires have burned in the valley several times since 2014." },
  "wolfville-ns": { original: "The Annapolis Valley lies between two parallel ridges of basalt and sandstone that shelter the valley floor from Atlantic fog and soften its maritime weather. The result is a long-settled fruit country — apple orchards since the 1600s, now with Nova Scotia's emerging cool-climate wine industry. Bay of Fundy tides and high summer sun give the valley enough heat to ripen fruit despite its northern latitude.", expanded: "Wolfville lies in the Annapolis Valley of Nova Scotia, on the shore of the Minas Basin, where the Bay of Fundy's tides are the highest in the world. The long ridge of North Mountain shelters the valley from the fog and cold wind off the bay. That shelter gives it some of the warmest summers in the province, with afternoons near 24°C, enough to ripen apples, which have been grown here since the 1600s, and now wine grapes. Winter is cold and snowy, with afternoons near 1°C, nights near −8°C and about 185 cm of snow. About 1300 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Autumn storms, sometimes the remains of hurricanes, are the main risk to the orchards." },
  "xilitla-mx": { original: "Xilitla sits on the windward Gulf slope of the Sierra Madre Oriental at 600 m. Moist Gulf air rises here and drips onto the forest almost continuously. The result is one of Mexico's lushest cloud forests, famous for the surreal sculpture garden Las Pozas.", expanded: "Xilitla sits at about 600 m on the slope of the Sierra Madre Oriental that faces the Gulf of Mexico, in the Huasteca region of San Luis Potosí. Moist air from the Gulf rises against the mountains and keeps the hillsides wet and densely forested. About 1520 mm of rain falls in a year, and September alone brings about 330 mm. It is warm and humid: afternoons average about 26°C in winter and 32°C in summer. In winter, cold fronts from the north bring days of mist and drizzle. The English poet Edward James built Las Pozas here, a garden of concrete towers and stairways among the waterfalls. Floods and landslides are the main hazards." },
  "yellowknife-nt": { original: "Yellowknife sits on Great Slave Lake's rocky north shore at 62.5° latitude. Winters are dark, dry, and brutal: January mean lows of −30°C, February sometimes colder. Great Slave Lake tempers the shore but freezes for half the year anyway. Summers are short, warm, and mosquito-saturated. The aurora is statistically the best in North America here.", expanded: "Yellowknife sits on bare rock on the north shore of Great Slave Lake, at about 62° north. Winter is long, dark and very cold: afternoons average about −18°C and nights about −27°C, and colder spells come every year. The air is dry and the sky often clear, which makes this one of the most reliable places in the world to see the aurora. The lake is frozen for about half the year. Summer is short and bright, with afternoons near 20°C and daylight nearly all night. Only about 290 mm of precipitation falls in a year. Wildfire is the summer risk. The whole city was evacuated in August 2023." },
  "zacatecas-mx": { original: "Zacatecas perches at 2440 m — 500 m higher than Morelia and in a more continental interior position. The result is a colder, drier eternal-spring climate: summer highs in the low 20s, winter lows that can reach −4°C, and annual precipitation under 500 mm. It's Mexico's most genuinely cool major historic city. The rose-pink stone, silver-mining history, and cultural intensity are all at altitude.", expanded: "Zacatecas lies at 2440 m in a ravine among dry hills, on the northern part of Mexico's central plateau. It is one of the highest and coolest cities in the country. Afternoons reach only about 19°C in winter and about 27°C in May, the warmest month. Winter nights fall to about 4°C, frost is not unusual, and the wind is cold. Only about 410 mm of rain falls in a year, most of it from June to September. The sky is clear for most of the year and the sun is strong at this height. The city was built from pink stone on the wealth of its silver mines. Drought is the main risk." },
};
